    "payload": {
      "wordMin": 180,
      "wordMax": 200,
      "caseNotes": "RAVENSFIELD DENTAL PRACTICE\nPatient: Miss Elodie Chukwuma-Sandoval, aged 9 years and 4 months\nAccompanied by: her father\nSeen: 12 January 2025 and 9 April 2025\nReferral written: 9 April 2025\n\nReason for referral:\nFailure of eruption of the upper left permanent central incisor, with evidence of an obstruction, in a child at an age where interceptive treatment is possible.\n\nHistory:\nThe upper right permanent central incisor erupted in March 2024, thirteen months ago.\nThe upper left permanent central incisor has not appeared.\nThe upper left deciduous central incisor was exfoliated in February 2024 — fourteen months ago — and the space has been empty since.\nBoth upper lateral incisors are unerupted, which is age-appropriate.\nHer father reports a fall onto the front teeth at the age of four, which was not seen by a dentist at the time. The deciduous tooth was chipped and became slightly grey afterwards.\n\nExamination, 9 April 2025:\nExtra-oral: no asymmetry, no lymphadenopathy, competent lips, no swelling.\nIntra-oral: upper left central incisor absent from the arch. No swelling or bulge palpable buccally; a firm fullness is palpable palatally in the region of the unerupted tooth.\nThe space for the tooth measures 8 mm; the erupted contralateral incisor measures 9 mm — so the space is 1 mm deficient and is beginning to close.\nUpper right central incisor: fully erupted, normal colour, responds to sensibility testing.\nDeciduous canines and molars present bilaterally, no mobility.\nOverjet 3 mm, overbite 40% and complete to tooth.\nClass I incisor relationship on the erupted side. No crossbite. Midline: upper midline displaced 1 mm to the left.\nOral hygiene good. No caries. Plaque score low.\n\nRadiographs, 9 April 2025:\nUpper standard occlusal and a periapical view taken.\nThe upper left central incisor is present, unerupted, and lies horizontally with the crown angled palatally and the incisal edge sitting above the apex of the upper right central incisor.\nRoot formation appears approximately two-thirds complete.\nA radiopacity consistent with a supernumerary tooth lies between the crown and the alveolar crest — this is the probable obstruction.\nNo evidence of resorption of the adjacent roots on these views, although I recognise the limits of plain films for that question.\nNo cyst formation seen.\n\nWhat has been done:\nJanuary 2025 — reviewed and a further three months allowed for spontaneous eruption, with the parents informed.\nApril 2025 — no change; radiographs taken and this referral made.\nSpace maintenance has not been attempted and I have not carried out any extraction.\n\nMedical history and cooperation:\nFit and well. No medication. No allergies. No previous general anaesthetic.\nShe is a good patient, accepted radiographs without difficulty, and was cooperative throughout both appointments.\nHer father is keen for treatment and has asked what the alternative would be if nothing is done.\nNeither parent has had orthodontic treatment. Her older brother, aged 13, is currently in fixed appliances at this practice.\n\nWhat the child and her father have said:\nElodie is aware of the gap and has begun covering her mouth when she smiles. Her father reports that she was asked about it at school in February and has been quieter since.\nShe asked directly at the last appointment whether the tooth \"was still in there\", and was shown the radiograph and told that it is.\nHer father asked whether waiting longer would help, and was told that the space is closing and that the tooth is unlikely to erupt on its own with an obstruction in its path.\nHe also asked whether the fall at the age of four caused this; he was told that trauma to a deciduous tooth can affect the one developing beneath it, that this is a plausible part of the history, and that it does not change what needs to be done now.\n\nPhotographs:\nIntra-oral photographs taken on 12 January and 9 April are enclosed, together with a measurement of the space on both dates, so that the 1 mm of closure is documented rather than described.\n\nRequest:\nAssessment with a view to removal of the supernumerary and exposure of the unerupted incisor, with whatever imaging your service prefers for the three-dimensional position.\nI would value your advice on whether space maintenance should be started here in the meantime, since the space has already reduced by 1 mm.\nHer father can bring her at any time and she attends the local primary school.",
      "recipient": "Mr Hugo Baptiste-Nwankwo, Consultant Orthodontist, Ravensfield Dental Hospital",
      "letterType": "referral",
      "taskInstruction": "Write a referral letter to the consultant orthodontist."
    }
