    "profession": "DENTISTRY",
    "slug": "wri-dentistry-referral-to-the-oral-surgery-department-extraction-in-a-patient-at-risk-of-jaw-necrosis",
    "title": "Dentistry — Referral to the oral surgery department: extraction in a patient at risk of jaw necrosis",
    "prompt": "Using the case notes, write a letter as instructed. Write 180–200 words. Select only the information that is relevant to the reader and to the purpose of the letter.",
    "difficulty": "CORE",
    "topicTag": "oral-surgery",
    "timeLimitSeconds": 2700,
    "active": true,
    "guidanceNote": "The tooth, the drug history and its duration, the reason the tooth cannot be saved, and the anticoagulant are the referral. His golf and the practice's new scanner are not.",
    "payload": {
      "wordMin": 180,
      "wordMax": 200,
      "caseNotes": "WEXLEY DENTAL PRACTICE\nPatient: Mr Aleksander Nwosu-Fairbairn\nAge: 71\nSeen: 5 and 19 July 2025\nReferral written: 19 July 2025\n\nReason for referral:\nExtraction required in a patient at elevated risk of medication-related osteonecrosis of the jaw, who is also anticoagulated.\n\nThe tooth and why it cannot be saved:\nLower right first molar.\nGross coronal breakdown with the caries extending subgingivally on the distal aspect to approximately 3 mm below the crest.\nPeriapical radiolucency of 6 mm at the distal root.\nGrade 2 mobility. Probing depth 9 mm distally with suppuration.\nFurcation involvement grade III.\nRoot canal treatment is not a realistic option: the remaining coronal tissue would not support a restoration and the periodontal support is inadequate. I have discussed this with him fully.\nHe has had three episodes of pain and swelling in eight months, the most recent on 30 June requiring antibiotics from his general practitioner.\nThere is no acute infection today: no swelling, no discharge, no trismus, no systemic upset.\n\nThe drug history — the reason for this referral:\nZoledronic acid by intravenous infusion, every six months, for metastatic prostate cancer.\nFirst infusion March 2022; most recent 12 June 2025; next due December 2025.\nThat is seven infusions over three years and four months.\nHe also received denosumab for eleven months in 2021 before the change of agent.\nNeither drug has been stopped and his oncology team have advised that neither should be interrupted.\nI have written to them and their reply is enclosed: they confirm the treatment is to continue and ask to be informed of any dental surgery.\n\nOther relevant medical history:\nMetastatic prostate cancer, bone metastases, on hormone treatment, stable disease at last review.\nAtrial fibrillation — apixaban 5 mg twice daily, not to be stopped without advice.\nType 2 diabetes — HbA1c 58 mmol/mol in May.\nPrevious radiotherapy: to the pelvis in 2020, none to the head or neck.\nImmunosuppression: none. Steroids: none currently.\nMedication: apixaban, bicalutamide, metformin, ramipril, atorvastatin, alendronic acid — NO, he is not on an oral bisphosphonate; the intravenous agent above is the only one.\nAllergies: none known.\n\nDental and oral status generally:\nOral hygiene is fair; he uses a manual brush twice daily and no interdental cleaning.\nRemaining dentition: 22 teeth. Two other teeth of questionable prognosis — the lower left second molar and the upper right second premolar — both currently asymptomatic and restorable, and I would value your view on whether they should be dealt with in the same episode.\nNo exposed bone anywhere. No non-healing socket. No previous extraction since the infusions began.\nRadiographs: an orthopantomogram taken 5 July is enclosed, together with a periapical of the tooth in question.\n\nWhat has been explained to him:\nThe nature of the risk to the jaw, in plain words, and that it is uncommon but serious.\nThat the tooth cannot be left because it is infected and will flare again.\nThat the extraction should be done where the risk can be managed rather than here.\nThat he must tell any dentist, now and in future, that he has had these infusions.\nHe has understood and has asked how long the risk lasts after the infusions stop.\n\nPreventive measures already in place:\nOral hygiene instruction given on 5 July, with an electric brush and interdental brushes supplied and demonstrated.\nHigh-fluoride toothpaste, 5000 parts per million, prescribed and started on 5 July.\nChlorhexidine mouthrinse for the ten days before any surgery, and he has been shown how to use it.\nHe has been placed on a three-monthly recall here for the rest of his treatment, rather than the usual interval, and the first of those is booked for October.\nHe has been advised against any extraction elsewhere, including abroad, and has agreed.\n\nHis own account:\nHe describes the pain as coming in waves, worst at night, and says he has been taking paracetamol \"most days\" since May, which is more than he had reported at the first appointment.\nHe is not frightened of the extraction itself; he is frightened of the jaw problem, which his oncology nurse mentioned to him in 2022 and which he has thought about ever since.\n\nRequest:\nExtraction of the lower right first molar under your care, with your assessment of the two questionable teeth at the same visit, and management of the anticoagulation according to your protocol.\nHe can attend at any time; his wife drives him.",
      "recipient": "Ms Farida Achike-Longstaff, Consultant Oral Surgeon, Wexley Dental Hospital",
      "letterType": "referral",
      "taskInstruction": "Write a referral letter to the consultant oral surgeon."
    }
