    "guidanceNote": "The measured loss, the intake, the symptoms elicited and the findings that need investigating are the referral. His pigeons and the community centre's timetable are not.",
    "payload": {
      "wordMin": 180,
      "wordMax": 200,
      "caseNotes": "WOLVERCOTE COMMUNITY DIETETIC SERVICE\nPatient: Mr Alaric Oyelaran-Bennett\nAge: 79\nSeen: 6 and 20 March 2025, at a community lunch club referral\nReferral written: 20 March 2025\n\nWhy he was referred to me:\nBy the lunch club coordinator, who noticed that his trousers no longer fit and that he had stopped finishing his meal. There was no medical referral and he has not seen a doctor since 2022.\n\nMeasured findings:\n6 March — weight 58.2 kg, height 1.75 m, body mass index 19.0.\n20 March — weight 56.9 kg.\nWeight recorded at the lunch club in October 2024 — 71 kg.\nLoss of 14.1 kg in five months — 19.9% of body weight.\nMid-upper arm circumference 21 cm. Handgrip 18 kg.\nMalnutrition screening score 4.\n\nIntake:\nApproximately 900 kcal daily.\nHe attends the lunch club twice weekly and eats about half of what is served.\nAt home: toast, tea, and a tinned pudding. He has stopped cooking.\nHe has stopped buying meat because \"it goes to waste\".\n\nSymptoms elicited on 20 March, on direct questioning — none of which he had reported to anybody:\nDifficulty swallowing solid food for approximately four months, worse with bread and meat, and improving with soft food. He described food \"sticking\" at the level of the lower sternum, and he now cuts everything very small and drinks water with every mouthful.\nNo pain on swallowing. No regurgitation of undigested food. No vomiting.\nHoarseness of the voice for about two months.\nA cough after drinking, occasionally.\nNo abdominal pain. No change in bowel habit. No blood in the stool.\nNo fever, no night sweats. No lumps that he has noticed.\nHe has not had a chest infection.\n\nExamination within my scope:\nThin, temporal wasting visible.\nNo lymphadenopathy palpable in the neck on the sides I could reach, though I did not examine the supraclavicular fossae properly and this needs doing.\nNo jaundice. No oedema. No obvious mass.\nVoice audibly hoarse throughout both appointments.\n\nOther history:\nLives alone since his wife died in 2012. No family locally.\nSmoker: twenty a day since the age of seventeen; still smoking.\nAlcohol: four to six units most evenings for many years.\nNo regular medication. No allergies. No previous surgery. No previous endoscopy.\nNo dentures; six teeth remaining. He raised this himself as the reason for the difficulty, which is a plausible explanation for part of it but not for the localisation to the lower sternum, the hoarseness, or the rate of the weight loss.\n\nWhat I have done and have not done:\nStarted fortification and supplements — two daily, supplied — and given soft high-energy advice.\nNOT treated this as a dental or a social problem, because progressive dysphagia to solids with a 19.9% weight loss, hoarseness and a lifelong smoking history is a pattern that needs investigating before it is managed.\nTelephoned your practice today; he has an appointment on 22 March.\nExplained to him why I am asking a doctor to see him. He asked whether it was serious and I told him honestly that it needed looking into and that I did not know.\n\nBloods:\nNone available. He has had no blood test since 2022, and I have not been able to arrange one.\n\nWhat the lunch club has observed, which is worth recording:\nHe used to arrive early and stay for the whole afternoon; since December he arrives late and leaves straight after the meal.\nHe has begun sitting alone rather than at the usual table, which the coordinator believes is because he is embarrassed about how slowly he now eats.\nHe declined the Christmas meal, which he had attended every year for eleven years.\n\nRequest:\nAssessment and consideration of an urgent suspected cancer referral for the upper gastrointestinal tract, and an ear nose and throat opinion for the hoarseness.\nHe has no transport and no family; the lunch club coordinator has offered to bring him and has his permission to do so.",
      "recipient": "Dr Rosalind Kilbride-Nnamdi, The Wolvercote Practice",
      "letterType": "referral",
      "taskInstruction": "Write a referral letter to the patient's general practitioner."
    }
