    "subTest": "WRITING",
    "taskType": "WRITING_LETTER",
    "profession": "DIETETICS",
    "slug": "wri-dietetics-letter-to-the-bariatric-team-assessment-before-surgery",
    "title": "Dietetics — Letter to the bariatric team: assessment before surgery",
    "prompt": "Using the case notes, write a letter as instructed. Write 180–200 words. Select only the information that is relevant to the reader and to the purpose of the letter.",
    "difficulty": "CORE",
    "topicTag": "bariatric-nutrition",
    "timeLimitSeconds": 2700,
    "active": true,
    "guidanceNote": "The weight history, the eating pattern, the comorbidities, the understanding and the concerns are the letter. Her sister's opinion and the clinic's waiting list are not.",
    "payload": {
      "wordMin": 180,
      "wordMax": 200,
      "caseNotes": "FARNWORTH COMMUNITY DIETETIC SERVICE\nPatient: Mrs Ottiline Wharton-Adeyemi\nAge: 44\nSeen: 3 and 24 May 2025, tier 3 weight management service\nLetter written: 24 May 2025\n\nReason for this letter:\nCompletion of the twelve-month tier 3 pathway, with my assessment for consideration of bariatric surgery.\n\nWeight history:\nHighest recorded weight, 2023 — 148 kg.\nOn entry to this service, May 2024 — 143 kg, body mass index 49.6, height 1.70 m.\nToday — 131 kg, body mass index 45.3. Loss of 12 kg, 8.4%, sustained over twelve months.\nWeight gain began after her second pregnancy in 2003 and accelerated after starting a night shift role in 2011.\nPrevious attempts: four commercial programmes, each with a loss of 8 to 15 kg followed by regain within a year; two courses of medication, both stopped for side effects.\n\nComorbidities:\nType 2 diabetes since 2020, HbA1c 62 on metformin and a GLP-1 agonist.\nObstructive sleep apnoea, on CPAP nightly, compliant with 6.5 hours average use.\nHypertension on two agents. Osteoarthritis of both knees. Fatty liver on ultrasound.\nReduced mobility: walks approximately 200 metres before knee pain stops her.\n\nEating pattern, assessed over the twelve months:\nNo binge eating disorder on structured assessment, repeated in March and April.\nNo purging. No night eating syndrome.\nGrazing pattern in the evenings, which she has substantially reduced.\nShe now eats three structured meals with two planned snacks, which she has maintained for seven months.\nFood diary completed accurately and consistently across the year; she has attended 19 of 20 appointments.\n\nBehavioural and psychological:\nAssessed by the service psychologist in November 2024 and again in April 2025.\nNo untreated depression; she had treatment for depression in 2012 and has been well since.\nNo alcohol misuse. Non-smoker.\nHer understanding of the procedure is good: she can describe the anatomical change, the expected loss, the lifelong supplementation, the risk of complications, and the fact that the operation does not treat the reasons she eats.\n\nHer understanding and expectations:\nShe expects to lose \"about a third\" of her weight, which is realistic.\nShe knows that regain is possible and that the operation is a tool rather than a cure.\nShe has asked about the effect on her diabetes and understands that improvement is likely but not guaranteed.\nShe has read about the vitamin requirements and knows they are lifelong; she was able to name the main ones without prompting.\n\nMy concerns, which I would rather set out than leave for you to find:\nHer home situation: she is the main carer for her mother, who has dementia, and she has twice cancelled appointments for this reason. The post-operative period will need practical planning.\nHer work: she remains on night shifts, and the post-operative eating pattern will be difficult across a twelve-hour night. She is unwilling to change shifts because of the pay.\nProtein intake is currently 55 g daily and would need to be substantially higher after surgery;\nthis needs work before rather than after.\nShe has not yet told her employer or her mother's social worker.\n\nPreoperative work completed:\nTwelve months of structured support, as above.\nVitamin screening: ferritin 18, vitamin D 34 nmol/L, B12 and folate normal.\nBoth are being corrected now rather than after surgery.\nLiver reduction diet explained and she has agreed to it for the two weeks before any operation.\n\nWhat she was asked and what she said:\nAsked why she wants the operation, she said: \"I want to be able to get down on the floor with my grandchildren and get back up again.\" She did not mention appearance at any point in twelve months.\nAsked what she would do if she lost no weight after the operation, she described the follow-up rather than blaming the surgery, which is a better answer than most.\nAsked what worries her, she said the vomiting and the possibility of not being able to eat with her family, both of which are reasonable and both of which are worth addressing at your assessment.\n\nPractical matters for your service:\nShe works twelve-hour night shifts, Sunday to Wednesday, and cannot easily attend a weekday morning appointment.\nShe has transport and lives 40 minutes from the hospital.\nShe has requested that her husband attend the assessment with her.\n\nMy view:\nShe meets the criteria, has engaged fully, has achieved and sustained a loss without surgery, and understands what she is asking for.\nI support the referral.",
      "recipient": "Ms Gwendolen Nkemelu-Rashford, Lead Dietitian, Bariatric Surgery Service, Farnworth Hospital",
      "letterType": "referral",
      "taskInstruction": "Write a letter to the lead bariatric dietitian setting out your assessment and your view."
    }
