    "subTest": "WRITING",
    "taskType": "WRITING_LETTER",
    "profession": "DIETETICS",
    "slug": "wri-dietetics-letter-to-the-general-practitioner-an-older-woman-on-a-restricted-diet",
    "title": "Dietetics — Letter to the general practitioner: an older woman on a restricted diet",
    "prompt": "Using the case notes, write a letter as instructed. Write 180–200 words. Select only the information that is relevant to the reader and to the purpose of the letter.",
    "difficulty": "CORE",
    "topicTag": "malnutrition",
    "timeLimitSeconds": 2700,
    "active": true,
    "guidanceNote": "The unnecessary restrictions, their origin, the resulting deficits and what you have changed are the letter. Her cat and the practice's appointment system are not.",
    "payload": {
      "wordMin": 180,
      "wordMax": 200,
      "caseNotes": "BARROWMEAD COMMUNITY DIETETIC SERVICE\nPatient: Mrs Hyacinth Delacroix-Mbeki\nAge: 83\nSeen: 12 and 26 June 2025, at home\nLetter written: 26 June 2025\n\nReason for this letter:\nMalnutrition in an older woman produced almost entirely by dietary restrictions that are no longer necessary, several of which were never necessary.\n\nThe restrictions she is following, and where each came from:\nLow fat — advised in 1991 after a cholesterol test. Never reviewed.\nLow salt — advised in 2004. Never reviewed.\nLow sugar — she has type 2 diabetes and was told in 2007 to \"avoid all sugar\", which she has interpreted as avoiding all puddings, all fruit and all milk.\nGluten free — started in 2012 on her daughter's advice after bloating. She has never been tested for coeliac disease and was never advised to do this by a clinician.\nLow potassium — she read a leaflet belonging to a neighbour who is on dialysis, concluded it applied to her, and stopped bananas, potatoes and tomatoes. Her kidney function is normal.\nDairy free — she stopped milk two years ago believing it caused her arthritis.\n\nWhat is left:\nBreakfast — a rice cake and black tea.\nLunch — a small tin of gluten-free soup.\nEvening — a piece of chicken and boiled carrots.\nNothing else. No snacks. No puddings. No milk. No fruit.\nEstimated intake 800 kcal and 35 g protein daily.\n\nConsequences, measured:\nWeight 44.2 kg on 12 June, 43.8 kg on 26 June. Height 1.60 m. Body mass index 17.1.\nWeight in 2022, from the practice record: 58 kg. Loss of 14.2 kg, 24%.\nHandgrip 12 kg. Malnutrition screening score 4.\nBloods, requested by me through your practice on 13 June: haemoglobin 98 with a low mean cell volume, ferritin 7, vitamin D 21 nmol/L, albumin 30, calcium 2.14, B12 low at 148, folate low.\nHbA1c 39 — she has not needed her metformin for some time and it may be contributing to the appetite and the B12.\nKidney function normal: creatinine 71, potassium 4.0, filtration rate 68.\nCoeliac serology, taken on gluten: not valid, as she has been gluten free for three years. This needs discussing rather than testing.\n\nWhat I have done:\nRemoved, with explanation and with her agreement, the low-potassium restriction and the low-fat restriction entirely.\nReintroduced full-fat milk, cheese, yoghurt and puddings, and explained why each is now appropriate.\nExplained that her HbA1c of 39 means the sugar restriction is doing harm rather than good at this weight, and agreed puddings twice daily.\nLeft the gluten restriction in place for now, because removing it before the coeliac question is settled would remove the ability to test her later, and I would value your view.\nStarted two supplement drinks daily.\nGiven a written plan of what to EAT, with quantities, and gone through it with her daughter.\n\nWhat she said, and why this happened:\nShe told me on 12 June: \"Every time I see somebody they tell me another thing to stop. Nobody has ever told me anything to start.\"\nShe has followed every piece of advice she has ever been given, precisely and for decades. She is the opposite of non-adherent, and the outcome is a body mass index of 17.1.\nShe was frightened when I put butter on her potatoes, and needed the reason explained twice before she would eat them.\n\nRequests:\nReview of the metformin, which she may no longer need and which may be contributing to the low B12 and the poor appetite.\nB12 and folate replacement, iron replacement, and vitamin D.\nYour decision on the coeliac question: whether a gluten challenge is appropriate at 83, or whether it is more sensible to leave the diet alone and treat her nutritionally.\nRepeat bloods in eight weeks.\nHer daughter has asked to be present at the next appointment and is supportive; several of the restrictions originated with her, she was not aware of the harm, and she has taken the explanation well rather than defensively.",
      "recipient": "Dr Aurelia Fitzwilliam-Okoro, The Barrowmead Practice",
      "letterType": "referral",
      "taskInstruction": "Write a letter to the patient's general practitioner setting out your findings and your requests."
    }
