    "guidanceNote": "The growth pattern, the intake, the feeding behaviour and what has already been tried are the referral. The family's holiday plans and the mother's employment history are not.",
    "payload": {
      "wordMin": 180,
      "wordMax": 200,
      "caseNotes": "ELLERSMERE COMMUNITY DIETETIC SERVICE — ADULT SERVICE\nPatient: Miss Amara Okonkwo-Balfour, aged 2 years and 5 months\nAccompanied by: her mother\nSeen: 12 January 2025, at a community clinic\nReferral written: 12 January 2025\n\nWhy she was seen by this service:\nShe was booked into an adult community dietetic clinic in error. Rather than cancel, I saw her and her mother, took a history, and am making this referral to the correct team.\n\nGrowth:\nBirth weight 3.1 kg, on the 50th centile.\n6 months — 7.4 kg, 50th centile.\n12 months — 9.2 kg, 25th centile.\n18 months — 9.8 kg, 9th centile.\n24 months — 10.1 kg, 2nd centile.\nToday, 29 months — 10.3 kg, below the 2nd centile.\nHeight today 88 cm, 25th centile. Height has continued along its line while weight has crossed three centile spaces.\nHead circumference: not measured here.\n\nIntake, from a three-day diary the mother had already been keeping:\nMilk: approximately 900 ml of cow's milk daily, mostly from a bottle, including two at night.\nMeals: she sits for less than two minutes and then leaves the table.\nEstimated intake from solid food is very small: two or three spoonfuls at most meals.\nSnacks: crisps and a fruit-flavoured yoghurt drink, offered whenever she asks.\nWater: almost none; she refuses it.\nTextures: she takes purees and very soft food only. She gags on anything lumpy and has vomited twice when a lump was given.\nShe has never eaten meat, bread or anything requiring chewing.\n\nFeeding behaviour:\nMeals have become a struggle. Her mother described chasing her around the room with a spoon and putting the television on to distract her.\nThe child turns her head away, clamps her mouth and cries at the sight of a bowl.\nShe will eat if she is given the bottle.\nNo food is eaten outside the home; she refuses at nursery, where she attends two days a week.\n\nWhat has already been tried:\nThe health visitor advised reducing milk in September; the mother reduced it for four days, the child \"screamed all night\", and it was restarted.\nA supplement drink was prescribed by the general practitioner in November; she refuses it.\nIron supplement prescribed in December after a haemoglobin of 96 with a low mean cell volume; the mother reports giving it \"most days\" and that it stains and is refused.\n\nRelevant history:\nBorn at term, no neonatal problems.\nWeaning started at 6 months and \"never really progressed\" beyond puree.\nRecurrent ear infections, four courses of antibiotics in the last year.\nNo vomiting other than with lumps. Bowels: opens every third day, hard stools, straining.\nNo diarrhoea, no blood in the stool, no rash, no wheeze.\nDevelopment otherwise: walking at 13 months, running, climbing, 30 to 40 words, follows instructions. No concern about her development from the mother, the nursery or from me today.\nImmunisations up to date. No medication apart from the iron.\nFamily: two older siblings, both well grown. No family history of coeliac disease or food allergy.\n\nWhat I have done:\nTaken the history above and calculated the intake.\nExplained why the milk volume is central to the problem, without asking her to change anything today.\nNOT started any plan, since this is not my patient group and a plan started here would need unpicking by your team.\nWritten to the general practitioner today to request a repeat full blood count, ferritin, and coeliac screening, and to ask about the constipation.\n\nRequest:\nPaediatric dietetic assessment, and I would suggest that a feeding therapy or speech and language opinion on the texture progression may also be needed, since the refusal of lumps at 29 months appears to be a skills problem as much as a behavioural one.\nHer mother is willing, has kept a diary unprompted, and is not resistant to advice — she has had three pieces of it and none has worked, which is different.\nShe is available on any weekday except Tuesday.",
      "recipient": "Ms Rosalind Fairbanks-Nwokolo, Paediatric Dietetic Team, Ellersmere Child Health Centre",
      "letterType": "referral",
      "taskInstruction": "Write a referral letter to the paediatric dietetic team."
    }
