    "timeLimitSeconds": 2700,
    "active": true,
    "guidanceNote": "The measured physical findings, the pattern of intake and exercise, the medical risk indicators and what the patient has said are the referral. Her university course details and her flatmate's opinion are not.",
    "payload": {
      "wordMin": 180,
      "wordMax": 200,
      "caseNotes": "ROTHBURY COMMUNITY DIETETIC SERVICE\nPatient: Miss Coralie Adebayo-Winstanley\nAge: 21\nSeen: 2 and 16 May 2025\nReferral written: 16 May 2025\n\nReason for referral:\nA young woman referred to dietetics for \"advice on healthy eating\" whose presentation requires specialist assessment rather than dietary advice.\n\nWhy she was referred to me:\nBy her general practitioner, for \"nutritional advice, low weight, wants to gain\".\n\nMeasured findings:\n2 May — weight 44.1 kg, height 1.68 m, body mass index 15.6.\n16 May — weight 43.4 kg, body mass index 15.4.\nWeight twelve months ago, from the practice record: 58 kg. Loss of 14.6 kg, 25% of body weight.\nBlood pressure 92/58 sitting, 78/50 standing at three minutes, with reported dizziness.\nPulse 44 at rest on both occasions.\nTemperature 35.7.\nSit-up-squat-stand test: unable to rise from squatting without using her arms, scoring 1 out of 3 on both components.\nHands and feet cold and blue. Fine downy hair on the forearms and the face.\nDry skin. Hair thinning, and she reports it coming out on the brush.\n\nIntake, from a three-day diary she completed herself:\nApproximately 700 to 900 kcal daily.\nNo fat of any kind. No carbohydrate after four in the afternoon. No food eaten in company.\nFoods are weighed. Portion sizes are recorded to the gram.\nShe reports \"safe\" foods and a list of foods she cannot eat, which has grown over eighteen months.\nNo binge eating reported. No vomiting reported. No laxative or diuretic use reported — I asked directly about all three.\nFluid: three litres of water and approximately eight cups of black coffee daily.\n\nExercise:\nRunning 12 to 14 kilometres daily, every day, including when unwell.\nAdditional home exercise, approximately 45 minutes, described as \"just stretching\" and then, on questioning, as including sit-ups and squats to a fixed count.\nShe has not missed a day since November and becomes distressed when asked what would happen if she did.\n\nWhat she has said:\nOn 2 May: \"I know I need to eat more, I just need someone to tell me exactly what.\"\nOn 16 May, when the plan from 2 May had not been followed: \"I couldn't do the oil. I can do everything else.\"\nShe describes a fear of \"losing control\" if she stops weighing food.\nShe said she has not had a period since September, which she attributes to her running.\nShe denies wanting to lose more weight and says she wants to be \"healthy and strong\".\n\nOther findings:\nBloods, 5 May: potassium 3.4, sodium 136, magnesium 0.68, phosphate 0.79, glucose 3.4.\nHaemoglobin 116. Albumin 39. Liver function normal. Thyroid function: low T3, normal TSH.\nECG, 9 May: sinus bradycardia at 44, corrected QT interval 470 ms.\nNo previous psychiatric history recorded. No self-harm on direct questioning. No thoughts of ending her life on direct questioning, and she agreed that this could be recorded and passed on.\n\nWhy I am referring rather than treating:\nThe presentation is not a knowledge problem, and a meal plan is not the treatment. She followed the first plan except for the one component that added fat, which is the component that carries the meaning.\nThe physical findings — pulse 44, corrected QT 470, postural drop, inability to rise from squatting, temperature 35.7 and a 25% weight loss — are medical risk indicators and are outside what a community dietitian should be holding alone.\n\nWhat I have done:\nTelephoned her general practitioner on 16 May; she is being seen on 18 May for a medical review and repeat ECG.\nGiven a small, achievable plan for the next fortnight, agreed with her, with no expectation that it resolves anything.\nExplained why I am referring and what the service does. She agreed and said she was \"relieved somebody had said it out loud\".\nAdvised her, and she has agreed, to reduce running to alternate days until she has been assessed — though I would not rely on that.\n\nRequest:\nUrgent assessment. I would draw attention to the ECG, the potassium and the postural readings.\nShe lives alone in student accommodation and her family live 200 miles away and do not know.",
      "recipient": "Dr Verity Nkemdi-Ashcroft, Consultant Psychiatrist, Community Eating Disorder Service, Rothbury",
      "letterType": "referral",
      "taskInstruction": "Write a referral letter to the community eating disorder service."
    }
