    "timeLimitSeconds": 2700,
    "active": true,
    "guidanceNote": "The symptoms, the biochemistry and antibodies, the eye findings, the treatment started and the pregnancy question are the referral. Her wedding and her sister's move abroad are not.",
    "payload": {
      "wordMin": 180,
      "wordMax": 200,
      "caseNotes": "BARROWMERE LANE SURGERY\nPatient: Miss Tanya Oduya-Whitfield\nAge: 31\nSeen: 7 and 21 March 2025\nReferral written: 21 March 2025\n\nReason for referral:\nNewly diagnosed thyrotoxicosis, biochemically and immunologically consistent with Graves' disease, with eye signs, in a woman planning a pregnancy.\n\nHistory:\nFour months of weight loss — eight kilograms, from 66 kg to 58 kg — despite an increased appetite;\nshe describes eating more than usual.\nPalpitations most days, described as a racing heart at rest, lasting minutes to hours.\nHeat intolerance, sweating, and sleeping with the window open in January while her partner complained of the cold.\nTremor of the hands, noticeable when holding a cup, for about three months.\nIrritability and tearfulness, which she attributes to stress at work; her partner disagrees and says it began before the work problems.\nLoose stools, three to four times daily, no blood, no nocturnal symptoms.\nFatigue and difficulty climbing stairs at work, which she describes as her legs rather than her breathing.\nMenstrual periods have become light and infrequent — two in five months, previously regular.\nEyes: gritty and watery for six weeks; her partner says her eyes \"look bigger\".\nNo double vision. No pain on eye movement. No loss of colour vision. No neck pain.\n\nExamination, 7 March:\nPulse 116, regular. Blood pressure 138/72. Weight 58 kg.\nFine tremor of the outstretched hands. Warm, moist palms. Brisk reflexes.\nThyroid: smoothly enlarged, approximately twice normal size, non-tender, no discrete nodule, a bruit audible over the gland.\nEyes: lid retraction bilaterally with visible sclera above the iris, lid lag on downgaze, mild proptosis, conjunctival injection. Full eye movements. No diplopia in any direction of gaze.\nVisual acuity 6/6 both eyes. Colour vision normal on testing.\nNo pretibial skin changes. No clubbing.\nECG, 7 March: sinus tachycardia at 114, no atrial fibrillation.\n\nInvestigations:\n7 March: TSH less than 0.01, free T4 42 pmol/L, free T3 18.2 pmol/L.\n21 March, repeated: TSH less than 0.01, free T4 39, free T3 17.4.\nTSH receptor antibodies: strongly positive.\nFull blood count normal, liver function normal, calcium normal, glucose normal.\nNo thyroid ultrasound or uptake scan performed; the antibody result and the clinical picture make the diagnosis, and I have left further imaging to your judgement.\n\nTreatment started, 21 March:\nPropranolol 40 mg three times daily for symptom control, started 7 March; pulse today 84 and the tremor is improved.\nCarbimazole 20 mg daily started today, 21 March, after discussion.\nShe has been given written warning about agranulocytosis: to stop the carbimazole immediately and seek a full blood count urgently if she develops a sore throat, mouth ulcers, fever or any unexplained illness. She repeated this back correctly.\nBaseline full blood count and liver function taken today before the first dose.\n\nThe pregnancy question — the reason for the urgency:\nShe and her partner have been trying to conceive for four months and she has continued to try.\nShe is not currently pregnant; a test today was negative and her last period was 4 March.\nShe has been advised to use reliable contraception until she has been seen and a plan agreed, because of the drug and because of the risks of uncontrolled thyrotoxicosis in pregnancy. She accepted this reluctantly and it is the thing she is most upset about.\n\nOther history and medication:\nNo other medical history. No previous thyroid disease. No previous surgery.\nFamily history: mother has hypothyroidism; a maternal aunt had Graves' disease treated with radioiodine.\nSmoker: ten a day for twelve years. She has been told that smoking worsens thyroid eye disease specifically, and she has agreed to a cessation referral, which has been made.\nNo other medication. Allergies: none known.\n\nRequest:\nAssessment at the thyroid clinic, with your advice on treatment strategy and on the timing of pregnancy, and on whether the eye changes warrant an ophthalmology opinion.\nShe works shifts as a paramedic and can attend on any weekday morning. She has asked whether she should be at work at all with a resting pulse in the hundreds, and I have said that this is a question for your clinic rather than one I should answer alone.",
      "recipient": "Dr Elias Mwangi, Consultant Endocrinologist, Thyroid Clinic, Barrowmere Hospital",
      "letterType": "referral",
      "taskInstruction": "Write a referral letter to the thyroid clinic."
    }
