    "guidanceNote": "The rhythm, the symptoms, the stroke and bleeding risk, what has already been started, and the thyroid and alcohol findings are the referral. His golf handicap and his son's wedding are not.",
    "payload": {
      "wordMin": 180,
      "wordMax": 200,
      "caseNotes": "BRIARWOOD MEDICAL PRACTICE\nPatient: Mr Roland Achebe-Marsh\nAge: 64\nSeen: 3 March 2025, 10 March 2025 and 17 March 2025\nReferral written: 17 March 2025\n\nPresenting history:\nFirst attended on 3 March describing episodes of \"fluttering\" in the chest, lasting between twenty minutes and several hours, occurring perhaps twice weekly for about four months and now daily.\nAssociated breathlessness on climbing stairs, which is new since January. No chest pain.\nOne episode of dizziness on 1 March while standing from a chair; no loss of consciousness, no fall.\nNo orthopnoea, no ankle swelling, no cough.\nExercise tolerance has fallen from two miles on the flat to about 400 metres.\n\nExamination, 3 March:\nPulse 118, irregularly irregular. Blood pressure 156/92, repeated 152/90.\nHeart sounds normal, no murmur. Chest clear. No peripheral oedema. No goitre palpable.\nWeight 94 kg, height 1.78 m, body mass index 29.7.\n\nInvestigations:\nECG 3 March: atrial fibrillation, ventricular rate 116, no acute ischaemic change, no pre-excitation.\nBloods 4 March: haemoglobin 148, platelets 232, creatinine 94, estimated filtration rate 76, potassium 4.4, magnesium normal, HbA1c 41, total cholesterol 5.8.\nThyroid function 4 March: TSH suppressed at 0.02, free T4 raised at 27. REPEATED 10 March and confirmed: TSH 0.02, free T4 26.\nThyroid antibodies sent 10 March, result pending.\nChest x-ray 5 March: normal heart size, clear lung fields.\nEchocardiogram requested 5 March; the appointment is 2 April and has not yet taken place.\nAmbulatory blood pressure monitoring 12–13 March: average daytime 148/90.\n\nRisk assessment:\nStroke risk score: 3 — age, hypertension, and the previous transient ischaemic attack below.\nBleeding risk score: 1 — hypertension only. No previous bleeding, no anaemia, no liver disease, no antiplatelet or anti-inflammatory use.\n\nRelevant history:\nTransient ischaemic attack in 2019 — right arm weakness and slurred speech for about forty minutes, fully resolved, investigated at Ashworth at the time, carotid dopplers normal.\nHypertension diagnosed 2017, treated. Type 2 diabetes excluded on the HbA1c above.\nNo previous cardiac disease. No rheumatic fever. Never smoked.\nAlcohol: he initially reported \"a few at the weekend\". On direct questioning on 10 March, using a structured screening tool, he reported eight to ten units on each of Friday, Saturday and Sunday, and two or three units on most other evenings — approximately 40 units weekly. He recognised the possible link when it was raised and has reduced it to about 15 units in the last week.\nFamily history: father had atrial fibrillation from his sixties; mother had a stroke aged 71.\n\nMedication:\nRamipril 10 mg daily — continued.\nAmlodipine 5 mg daily — started 3 March for the blood pressure.\nBisoprolol 2.5 mg daily — started 3 March for rate control, increased to 5 mg on 10 March.\nApixaban 5 mg twice daily — started 4 March after discussion of the stroke and bleeding risks;\nhe understood the reasoning and agreed. He has taken it without problems.\nAtorvastatin 20 mg at night — started 10 March.\nAllergies: none known.\n\nResponse so far:\nPulse on 17 March: 88, still irregular. Blood pressure 138/84.\nHe reports the palpitations are less noticeable but still present daily. Breathlessness unchanged.\nHe has not had further dizziness.\n\nReason for referral:\nSymptomatic atrial fibrillation, persistent despite rate control, in a man of 64 with a previous transient ischaemic attack and with untreated thyrotoxicosis identified on screening. He is a candidate for consideration of rhythm control, and I would value your opinion on the sequence — whether the thyroid abnormality should be treated first, and whether cardioversion or ablation should be considered once he is euthyroid.\nAn urgent endocrinology referral was made on 10 March; he has not yet been seen.\nThe echocardiogram is booked for 2 April and I have not delayed this referral for it.\n\nSocial:\nLives with his wife. Works full time as a quantity surveyor and drives approximately 20,000 miles a year for work. Non-smoker.\n\nPatient's own questions:\nHe has asked whether he can continue to drive, whether he will need to take the anticoagulant for life, and whether stopping alcohol altogether would remove the need for any of this.",
      "recipient": "Dr Marcus Vaillancourt, Consultant Cardiologist, Ashworth General Hospital",
      "letterType": "referral",
      "taskInstruction": "Write a referral letter to the consultant cardiologist requesting assessment."
    }
