    "slug": "wri-medicine-discharge-to-the-general-practitioner-diabetic-ketoacidosis-at-first-presentation",
    "title": "Medicine — Discharge to the general practitioner: diabetic ketoacidosis at first presentation",
    "prompt": "Using the case notes, write a letter as instructed. Write 180–200 words. Select only the information that is relevant to the reader and to the purpose of the letter.",
    "difficulty": "CORE",
    "topicTag": "endocrinology",
    "timeLimitSeconds": 2700,
    "active": true,
    "guidanceNote": "The presentation, the treatment, the insulin regimen, the education given, the driving and the follow-up are the letter. The details of the ward's bed moves and the patient's university course are not.",
    "payload": {
      "wordMin": 180,
      "wordMax": 200,
      "caseNotes": "BROADMEAD ROYAL INFIRMARY — ACUTE MEDICAL UNIT AND WARD 3\nPatient: Miss Yusra Al-Habib\nAge: 19\nAdmitted: 8 May 2024\nDischarged: 13 May 2024\n\nPresentation:\nThree weeks of thirst, passing urine frequently including at night, and weight loss of approximately seven kilograms. Two days of vomiting and abdominal pain. Brought in by her flatmate.\n\nOn arrival:\nDrowsy but rousable. Respiratory rate 30, deep breathing. Pulse 124. Blood pressure 96/58.\nTemperature 36.9. Dry mucous membranes, reduced skin turgor.\nCapillary glucose 31.4 mmol/L. Capillary ketones 5.8 mmol/L.\nVenous blood gas: pH 7.09, bicarbonate 8, base excess minus 20.\nSodium 132, potassium 5.2 on arrival, creatinine 118, urea 11.2.\nDiagnosis: diabetic ketoacidosis at first presentation of type 1 diabetes.\n\nTreatment:\nFixed-rate intravenous insulin infusion with intravenous fluids and potassium replacement according to protocol, from 22:40 on 8 May.\nHourly glucose and ketone monitoring; potassium checked at one, two, four, eight, twelve and twenty-four hours.\nKetosis resolved by 15:00 on 9 May, pH 7.36, bicarbonate 21, ketones 0.4.\nTransferred to subcutaneous insulin at 18:00 on 9 May with the overlap maintained.\nNo hypoglycaemia at any point. No cerebral oedema. No thrombotic complication.\nCreatinine returned to 74 by 10 May.\nPrecipitant: none identified. No infection found — chest x-ray clear, urine culture negative, no diarrhoea. This was a first presentation rather than an omission of treatment.\n\nConfirmatory investigations:\nHbA1c on admission 108 mmol/mol.\nGAD antibodies positive. C-peptide low. Coeliac screening negative. Thyroid function normal.\n\nInsulin regimen on discharge:\nBasal: insulin degludec 14 units at 22:00.\nBolus: insulin aspart with meals, using a starting ratio of 1 unit for every 10 grams of carbohydrate, and a correction factor of 1 unit for every 3 mmol/L above 8.\nShe has given every injection herself since 11 May and her technique has been checked twice.\nBlood glucose monitoring: flash sensor fitted 12 May, plus finger-prick meter and strips supplied.\nKetone meter and strips supplied.\n\nEducation completed on the ward:\nInjection technique and site rotation. Carbohydrate counting, first session completed and further sessions booked. Recognition and treatment of hypoglycaemia, with a hypoglycaemia kit supplied.\nSick day rules — never omit basal insulin, check ketones, fluids, and when to seek help — given in writing and repeated on 12 May.\nDriving: she holds a provisional licence and does not yet drive. She has been told she must inform the licensing authority before she begins to drive, and about testing before and during driving.\nAlcohol and delayed hypoglycaemia discussed; she reports drinking on Friday nights with friends.\n\nDischarge state:\nGlucose values 6.4 to 11.2 over the last 24 hours. Ketones negative. Eating normally.\nWeight 51 kg. Blood pressure 112/70.\nEmotionally, she has been tearful twice, mainly about telling her parents, who live abroad. She declined psychology input on the ward and agreed to be asked again at clinic.\n\nSocial circumstances relevant to her care:\nLives in shared student accommodation with three others; her flatmate has been shown how to use the glucagon kit and has agreed to this. Parents live in Amman; she has not yet told them and has asked that no letter be sent to her home address. She works two evening shifts a week in a bar.\nShe has no cooking facilities beyond a shared kitchen and eats mainly at the university canteen, which is relevant to the carbohydrate counting.\n\nFollow-up arranged:\nDiabetes specialist nurse telephone review 15 May, and face to face 22 May.\nConsultant diabetes clinic 6 June.\nStructured education course booked for September.\nRetinal screening and foot screening to be arranged by the practice within three months.\n\nProgress on the ward:\n10 May — fully alert, eating, mobilising independently. Reviewed by the diabetes specialist nurse.\n11 May — first self-administered injection under supervision, then all subsequent doses herself.\n12 May — sensor fitted; she was able to interpret the trend arrows correctly when tested.\n13 May — reviewed by the consultant; discharge agreed. She asked to go home rather than stay for a further day of education and this was accepted, with the telephone review brought forward to 15 May.\n\nRequests to the practice:\nAdd to the diabetes register and issue repeat prescriptions for insulin, needles, sensors, meters, strips and glucagon. Annual review from May 2025. Influenza vaccination each autumn.\nNo other medication. Allergies: none known.",
      "recipient": "Dr Susan Pargeter, The Broadmead Surgery",
      "letterType": "discharge",
      "taskInstruction": "Write a discharge letter to the patient's general practitioner."
    }
