    "timeLimitSeconds": 2700,
    "active": true,
    "guidanceNote": "The severity score, the trajectory, the oxygen requirement, the antibiotics with times, the ceiling of treatment discussion and the outstanding results are the transfer. The bed pressures on the medical ward and the patient's caravan are not.",
    "payload": {
      "wordMin": 180,
      "wordMax": 200,
      "caseNotes": "KELVINSIDE HOSPITAL — MEDICAL WARD 4\nPatient: Mr Alasdair MacQuarrie\nAge: 68\nAdmitted: 11 January 2025, 02:15\nTransferring: 12 January 2025, 09:40\n\nPresentation:\nFour days of fever, cough productive of rust-coloured sputum, and pleuritic right-sided chest pain, with two days of increasing breathlessness. Brought in by ambulance after his wife found him confused in the bathroom.\n\nOn admission, 02:15:\nTemperature 38.9. Respiratory rate 34. Pulse 118. Blood pressure 92/54.\nOxygen saturation 88% on air, 94% on 40% oxygen.\nConfused — abbreviated mental test 7 out of 10, normally fully orientated.\nUrea 9.4. Severity score 4 out of 5, indicating severe pneumonia.\nChest: bronchial breathing and coarse crackles right base and mid-zone, dull to percussion.\nChest x-ray: dense right lower and middle lobe consolidation, small right pleural effusion.\nBloods: white cells 22.8 with neutrophilia, C-reactive protein 348, creatinine 142 (baseline 88), lactate 3.2, platelets 108, bilirubin 28, albumin 24.\nBlood gas on 40% oxygen: pH 7.44, pO2 8.1, pCO2 3.9, bicarbonate 20.\n\nTreatment given, with times:\n02:35 — blood cultures taken, two sets.\n02:40 — intravenous co-amoxiclav and clarithromycin commenced.\n02:45 — 500 ml crystalloid bolus, repeated at 03:20 and 04:10, total 1500 ml.\n04:30 — urinary catheter inserted for hourly output measurement.\n06:00 — lactate 2.1, blood pressure 104/62, urine output 35 ml/hour.\nContinued oxygen to maintain saturations above 94%; no history of carbon dioxide retention.\n\nProgress over the first 30 hours:\n11 January, 14:00 — less confused, mental test 9 out of 10. Oxygen requirement 40%.\n11 January, 22:00 — respiratory rate 30, saturations 93% on 60% oxygen. Blood pressure 98/58.\n12 January, 04:00 — increasing work of breathing. Oxygen increased to 15 litres via a reservoir mask, saturations 91%. Repeat gas: pH 7.47, pO2 7.6 on high-flow oxygen, pCO2 3.6.\n12 January, 06:30 — repeat chest x-ray: extension of consolidation to the right upper zone and a new small area at the left base. Effusion unchanged.\n12 January, 07:00 — repeat bloods: white cells 26.1, C-reactive protein 402, creatinine 178, platelets 92, lactate 2.8.\n12 January, 08:00 — antibiotics escalated to piperacillin-tazobactam after microbiology advice;\nclarithromycin continued. Legionella and pneumococcal urinary antigens sent, results pending.\n12 January, 09:00 — reviewed by the medical consultant. Type 1 respiratory failure not responding to standard oxygen therapy; referred for high dependency care.\n\nCurrent state at transfer:\nRespiratory rate 32. Saturations 91% on 15 litres. Pulse 112. Blood pressure 100/60.\nUrine output 25 ml/hour over the last four hours. Alert, orientated, able to give consent.\nPeripherally shut down. No rash. No calf swelling.\n\nRelevant history:\nCOPD, moderate, on inhalers; never required non-invasive ventilation, never admitted with COPD.\nEx-smoker, stopped 2013, 40 pack-year history. Hypertension. Type 2 diabetes on metformin, which has been withheld since admission. No cardiac history.\nFunctional baseline: independent, walks two miles with his dog, drives, no cognitive impairment.\nAllergies: none known.\n\nTreatment escalation discussion:\nDiscussed with the patient at 09:10 on 12 January, in the presence of his wife, while he was fully orientated. He wishes to receive all treatment including ventilation if it is required and if it is thought likely to help. He asked to be told honestly if it were not.\nHis wife holds no formal authority and he has no advance decision.\n\nOutstanding:\nBlood cultures — no growth at 24 hours, ongoing. Sputum culture sent 11 January. Urinary antigens pending. Repeat lactate and gas due at 10:00. Pleural fluid not sampled; the effusion is small and was not considered accessible without ultrasound guidance.\n\nLines and access:\nTwo peripheral cannulae, 18 gauge left antecubital fossa and 20 gauge right forearm, both sited 11 January and both patent. No central access. Urinary catheter as above, sited 04:30 on 11 January. Venous thromboembolism prophylaxis given from 06:00 on 11 January and the dose has been reduced today because of the falling platelet count and rising creatinine.\n\nNutrition and glucose:\nNil by mouth since 04:00 today because of the work of breathing. Capillary glucose 4 to 8 hourly, range 9.2 to 14.6, sliding scale not started. Last glucose 11.8 at 08:00.\n\nRequests:\nTransfer for organ support and consideration of non-invasive respiratory support. Hourly urine output continues. Please note the metformin remains withheld and the creatinine is rising.\nHis wife is on the ward now and would like to speak to your team before she goes home.",
      "recipient": "Dr Anwar Faisal, Consultant in Intensive Care Medicine, High Dependency Unit, Kelvinside Hospital",
      "letterType": "transfer",
      "taskInstruction": "Write a transfer letter to the consultant in intensive care medicine."
    }
