    "guidanceNote": "The retinal findings, the vision, the diabetes control, the screening history and what the patient understands are the referral. Her hairdressing business and the practice's opening hours are not.",
    "payload": {
      "wordMin": 180,
      "wordMax": 200,
      "caseNotes": "ELLENFORD OPTOMETRY PRACTICE\nPatient: Mrs Yolanda Okereke-Bassett\nAge: 52\nExamined: 3 November 2025\nReferral written: 3 November 2025\n\nReason for referral:\nSight-threatening diabetic retinopathy found at a routine sight test, in a patient who has not attended diabetic eye screening for four years.\n\nVision:\nRight 6/9 unaided, 6/9 best corrected.\nLeft 6/18 unaided, 6/12 best corrected — reduced from 6/6 in 2021.\nShe reports that the left eye has been \"a bit blurred\" for about six months and had assumed she needed new glasses.\nNo distortion reported when asked, but she was positive on an Amsler grid in the left eye today, describing a small area of waviness just off centre.\n\nDilated fundus findings:\nRIGHT: scattered dot and blot haemorrhages in all four quadrants, several cotton wool spots, and definite venous beading in one quadrant. No new vessels seen at the disc or elsewhere.\nAssessment: severe non-proliferative retinopathy.\nLEFT: as above, plus new vessels elsewhere in the inferotemporal arcade, approximately half a disc area, with no vitreous haemorrhage.\nAssessment: proliferative retinopathy.\nBoth eyes: hard exudates within one disc diameter of the fovea, more marked on the left, with associated retinal thickening on the left.\nOptical coherence tomography: central macular thickness right 290 microns, left 412 microns with intraretinal cysts — clinically significant macular oedema on the left.\nImages and scans of both eyes are attached.\n\nAnterior segment and pressures:\nBoth eyes quiet. No rubeosis at the pupil margin on either side, checked before dilation.\nPressures 16 and 17. Gonioscopy not performed.\nLenses: early cortical changes both eyes, not visually significant.\n\nDiabetes history:\nType 2 diabetes, diagnosed 2007.\nHbA1c, from her own record on her telephone: 89 mmol/mol in August 2025, 84 in February 2025.\nOn metformin and gliclazide; a GLP-1 agonist was started in September.\nBlood pressure measured here today: 168/96 and 164/94 on repeat — I have written to her general practitioner separately about this today.\nCholesterol: she does not know. Kidney function: she does not know.\nSmoker, ten a day.\n\nScreening history, which is the reason this was found today rather than in 2022:\nShe last attended diabetic eye screening in 2021.\nShe has received invitations and has not attended, and told me she \"did not think it mattered because her eyes were fine\".\nShe has not seen an optometrist since 2021 either; she came today only because her reading glasses had broken.\n\nWhat I have explained to her:\nThat the changes at the back of both eyes are caused by the diabetes.\nThat the left eye has new blood vessels growing, which can bleed, and that this needs treatment rather than monitoring.\nThat the blurring in the left eye is swelling at the centre of the retina and that this is also treatable.\nThat treatment protects the vision she has and does not usually restore vision already lost, which she found hard and which I said plainly rather than softening.\nShe asked whether she would go blind. I told her that the risk is real, that this is why the referral is urgent, and that treatment substantially reduces it.\n\nOther ocular history:\nNo previous eye disease. No family history of glaucoma or macular degeneration.\nNo previous laser or injections. Refraction: right plus 1.25, left plus 1.50, with a reading addition of plus 2.00.\nNo ocular medication. Allergies: none known.\n\nWhat I have already done:\nExplained the findings with the images on the screen in front of her, which she found more convincing than any description.\nWritten to her general practitioner today about the blood pressure of 168/96, the HbA1c and the smoking.\nBooked her back here for a review in six months regardless of the hospital appointment, so that she does not disappear from both systems again.\nGiven her the leaflet and written down what she should do if the vision changes suddenly or if she sees a shower of floaters, and told her that a sudden change means the emergency department rather than an appointment.\n\nRequest:\nUrgent assessment — proliferative diabetic retinopathy in the left eye with clinically significant macular oedema, and severe non-proliferative changes in the right.\nShe has agreed and understands that she must attend.\nShe works Tuesday to Saturday and can attend on a Monday or after five o'clock.",
      "recipient": "Dr Marguerite Okonjo-Hartfield, Consultant Ophthalmologist, Medical Retina Service, Ellenford Hospital",
      "letterType": "referral",
      "taskInstruction": "Write a referral letter to the consultant ophthalmologist."
    }
