    "subTest": "WRITING",
    "taskType": "WRITING_LETTER",
    "profession": "OPTOMETRY",
    "slug": "wri-optometry-referral-to-the-glaucoma-service",
    "title": "Optometry — Referral to the glaucoma service",
    "prompt": "Using the case notes, write a letter as instructed. Write 180–200 words. Select only the information that is relevant to the reader and to the purpose of the letter.",
    "difficulty": "CORE",
    "topicTag": "glaucoma",
    "timeLimitSeconds": 2700,
    "active": true,
    "guidanceNote": "The repeated measurements, the discs, the fields, the family history and the exclusions are the referral. The patient's caravan and the practice's equipment purchase are not.",
    "payload": {
      "wordMin": 180,
      "wordMax": 200,
      "caseNotes": "THORNHILL OPTOMETRY PRACTICE\nPatient: Mrs Ottilie Fennimore-Adeyinka\nAge: 61\nExamined: 8 February and 1 March 2025\nReferral written: 1 March 2025\n\nReason for referral:\nRepeatable visual field loss with corresponding disc changes and raised intraocular pressure in both eyes, consistent with chronic open angle glaucoma.\n\nIntraocular pressures, all by Goldmann applanation:\n8 February — right 29, left 27, at 10:20.\n1 March — right 31, left 28, at 15:40.\nRepeated at both visits and consistent.\nCentral corneal thickness: right 512, left 508 microns — thin, which means the true pressures are likely to be HIGHER than the readings above rather than lower.\n\nOptic discs, dilated:\nRight: cup to disc ratio 0.8 vertically, with thinning of the inferior neuroretinal rim and a notch at seven o'clock. A splinter haemorrhage at the inferior disc margin — present on 8 February and still present on 1 March.\nLeft: cup to disc ratio 0.7 with inferior rim thinning. No haemorrhage.\nDisc size: both average. Asymmetry between the two discs of 0.1, which is itself significant.\nPhotographs of both discs from both visits are enclosed.\n\nVisual fields, automated threshold, both eyes, both visits:\nRight: a superior arcuate defect respecting the horizontal midline, mean deviation minus 6.8 dB.\nLeft: an early superior nasal step, mean deviation minus 2.4 dB.\nReliability good on all four tests: fixation losses under 10%, false positives under 5%.\nThe right defect on 1 March matches that of 8 February in position and depth.\n\nOther findings:\nGonioscopy: angles open to the ciliary body in all quadrants, both eyes. No angle recession, no pigment, no peripheral anterior synechiae.\nAnterior segments quiet. No pseudoexfoliation seen at the pupil margin or on the lens after dilation.\nNo iris transillumination.\nOptical coherence tomography, retinal nerve fibre layer: right inferior quadrant 62 microns, left inferior 78 — both outside normal limits, right more than left, matching the fields and the discs.\nVision: right 6/6, left 6/6 corrected. Refraction: right minus 1.50, left minus 1.75.\nLenses clear. Maculae normal.\n\nRisk factors and history:\nFamily history: her mother was registered sight impaired from glaucoma at 71, and her brother is under treatment. This is the reason she attends regularly and it is the reason this has been found at a treatable stage rather than later.\nNo diabetes. No hypertension. No migraine. No Raynaud's phenomenon.\nNo steroid use of any kind, checked specifically, including inhalers, drops, creams and injections.\nNo previous eye trauma. No previous eye surgery. No uveitis.\nMedication: levothyroxine only. Allergies: none known.\nNon-smoker.\n\nWhat I have and have not done:\nNo treatment has been started; I have not prescribed any drops, as the diagnosis and the target pressure are for your service and untreated pressures are more useful to you.\nExplained the diagnosis to her in plain terms: what glaucoma is, that it is the optic nerve rather than the eye's surface, that the loss already present will not return, and that treatment is to protect what remains.\nExplained that it is usually painless and that this is why it is found by testing rather than by symptoms — she said she had \"no idea anything was wrong\", which is exactly the point.\nExplained that her children should be tested from the age of 40, and free of charge given the family history, and she has agreed to tell them.\nAdvised her that this does not currently affect her driving, and that this may change and will be assessed formally.\n\nHer own questions, which I could not fully answer:\nWhether the loss already present will come back — I told her it will not, plainly.\nWhether she will go blind — I told her that with treatment and monitoring most people do not, that her mother's generation was diagnosed later and treated with less, and that this is a question worth asking your team with her own numbers in front of her.\nWhether drops for life — I said that this is the usual answer and that the choice of treatment is yours to make.\n\nRequest:\nAssessment at the glaucoma service. The disc haemorrhage present at both visits, the thin corneas and the arcuate defect in the right eye together suggest this should be seen sooner rather than at a routine interval.\nShe can attend any weekday and has transport.",
      "recipient": "Mr Silas Okoro-Vandermeer, Consultant Ophthalmologist, Glaucoma Service, Thornhill Hospital",
      "letterType": "referral",
      "taskInstruction": "Write a referral letter to the consultant ophthalmologist."
    }
