    "subTest": "WRITING",
    "taskType": "WRITING_LETTER",
    "profession": "OPTOMETRY",
    "slug": "wri-optometry-referral-to-the-low-vision-service",
    "title": "Optometry — Referral to the low vision service",
    "prompt": "Using the case notes, write a letter as instructed. Write 180–200 words. Select only the information that is relevant to the reader and to the purpose of the letter.",
    "difficulty": "CORE",
    "topicTag": "low-vision",
    "timeLimitSeconds": 2700,
    "active": true,
    "guidanceNote": "The measured vision, the tasks she has lost, the equipment already tried and the home circumstances are the referral. Her late husband's career and the practice's stock are not.",
    "payload": {
      "wordMin": 180,
      "wordMax": 200,
      "caseNotes": "CRANMERE OPTOMETRY PRACTICE\nPatient: Mrs Adaeze Trelawney-Iwu\nAge: 84\nExamined: 9 July 2025\nReferral written: 9 July 2025\n\nReason for referral:\nStable but substantially reduced vision from macular degeneration, with a loss of the specific tasks that matter to her, for low vision assessment and rehabilitation.\n\nVision and stability:\nRight 6/60, left 3/60, best corrected. Both eyes.\nNear vision: N24 with a plus 4.00 addition, right eye, at 15 cm.\nThis has been stable across four examinations over eighteen months: 6/60 and 3/60 on each occasion.\nBoth eyes have end-stage neovascular macular degeneration with disciform scarring; she completed three years of injections and was discharged from the macular service in March 2025 as stable and not amenable to further treatment. Their discharge letter is enclosed.\nPeripheral fields are full to confrontation in both eyes.\nNo cataract of significance; both eyes pseudophakic since 2017.\n\nWhat she can and cannot do — assessed rather than reported:\nCannot read standard print at all, including her own post and her medication labels.\nCannot see faces across a room; she recognises people by voice and by gait.\nCan see large objects, doorways, furniture and steps, and mobilises safely indoors.\nCannot read a bus number, so she has stopped using buses.\nCannot see the cooker dials.\nCan watch television if she sits at one metre, which she does.\nReads large-print library books with a hand magnifier \"when the light is good\", slowly and for about ten minutes before fatigue.\n\nEquipment already tried and what happened:\nAn 8-dioptre hand magnifier bought privately in 2023 — she uses it and it is now insufficient.\nA stand magnifier borrowed from a friend, abandoned because she could not position it.\nNo task lighting anywhere in the house; her main light is a central ceiling fitting.\nNo electronic magnifier tried.\nNo talking devices, no audio books, no telephone with large buttons.\nShe has never been assessed by a low vision service and has never been registered.\n\nThe tasks she has lost, in her own words, which are the goals for your assessment:\nReading her own letters, particularly anything from the hospital or the council.\nDoing the crossword, which she did daily for sixty years.\nSeeing her great-grandchildren's faces in photographs.\nCooking, which she has stopped since scalding her hand in February.\nReading her own medication labels; her daughter now fills a weekly box.\n\nHome and circumstances:\nLives alone in a first-floor flat with a lift.\nDaughter visits twice weekly and does the shopping.\nNo care package. No falls in the last year.\nCognitively intact; she manages her own money with her daughter's help and gave a full, accurate history.\nShe is not registered as sight impaired, and I have discussed certification with her today.\nShe has agreed to be certified and I have asked the eye unit to arrange it, as the certificate opens the practical support she is currently not receiving.\n\nWhat she said, and why this referral is overdue:\nShe told me today that she had assumed that \"nothing more can be done\" meant nothing at all, and that she had stopped asking.\nShe has been discharged as stable and has been left with no rehabilitation, which is the gap this referral is intended to close.\nHer vision is stable. Her function has fallen a long way further than her vision has, and that difference is what your service can address.\n\nOther information relevant to your assessment:\nHearing: good; she wears no aid and heard normal conversation without difficulty.\nHands: mild arthritis, which affects her ability to hold a hand magnifier steadily for more than a few minutes and which is relevant to the choice of device.\nMood: she described herself as \"fed up rather than depressed\" and declined any further discussion of it; it is worth revisiting.\nShe has no computer and no smartphone, and has said she is \"too old to start\", which may or may not be the last word on it.\n\nRequest:\nLow vision assessment, including magnification for reading, task lighting advice, and a home visit if your service offers one — she will not manage a clinic assessment of lighting in her own kitchen.\nShe is available every day except Thursday and can be collected by her daughter at weekends.",
      "recipient": "Ms Perpetua Halloran-Nwachukwu, Lead, Low Vision Service, Cranmere Eye Unit",
      "letterType": "referral",
      "taskInstruction": "Write a referral letter to the low vision service lead."
    }
