    "guidanceNote": "The four discrepancies, the evidence for each, what you have already done and what you are asking the practice to do are the letter. The pharmacy's delivery rota and the patient's grandchildren are not.",
    "payload": {
      "wordMin": 180,
      "wordMax": 200,
      "caseNotes": "BRIDGEWATER COMMUNITY PHARMACY — MEDICINES RECONCILIATION SERVICE\nPatient: Mrs Norah Kilfeather-Duru\nAge: 79\nSeen: 18 and 25 October 2025, both at home during a delivery visit\nLetter written: 25 October 2025\n\nBackground:\nDischarged from Selby General Hospital on 12 October after an eleven-day admission with a fall and a urinary tract infection. She lives alone, uses a weekly dosette box supplied by this pharmacy, and her daughter collects and checks it each Monday.\nThe discharge summary reached this pharmacy on 16 October. The practice issued a repeat prescription on 17 October.\n\nReason for this letter:\nFour discrepancies between the discharge summary, the practice's repeat list and what the patient is actually taking, all identified on 18 October and confirmed on 25 October.\n\nDiscrepancy 1 — a drug stopped in hospital that is still being supplied.\nThe discharge summary states: \"Amitriptyline 25 mg at night — STOPPED, contributed to fall\".\nThe practice's repeat list issued on 17 October still contains amitriptyline 25 mg at night.\nMrs Kilfeather-Duru has continued to take it every night since discharge, from a box she already had at home, and it was included in the dosette box I dispensed on 17 October.\nI have removed it from the box prepared on 25 October and explained the reason to her and to her daughter. She has agreed to stop and has given me the remaining supply.\n\nDiscrepancy 2 — a dose change not carried across.\nThe discharge summary states furosemide 20 mg each morning, reduced from 40 mg because of the kidney function during admission.\nThe practice's repeat list and the prescription issued on 17 October both state 40 mg.\nShe has taken 40 mg every morning since discharge — thirteen days at twice the intended dose.\nHer creatinine on discharge was 128, and I have not had access to any result since.\n\nDiscrepancy 3 — a drug started in hospital that has not reached the repeat list.\nThe discharge summary states nitrofurantoin was completed as a treatment course and that trimethoprim 100 mg at night was to be started as prophylaxis for three months from 12 October.\nThis does not appear on the repeat list and has never been dispensed. She is not taking it and did not know it had been prescribed.\n\nDiscrepancy 4 — a formulation issue.\nShe has been supplied with alendronic acid 70 mg weekly tablets. On direct questioning she takes it with her breakfast, sitting down, with tea, and then goes back to bed.\nThis has been the pattern since 2021. She has not been taking it as directed at any point and reports intermittent heartburn, for which she buys an antacid.\n\nWhat the patient is actually taking, verified from the boxes in her kitchen on 25 October:\nAmlodipine 5 mg daily — as intended.\nFurosemide 40 mg each morning — should be 20 mg.\nAmitriptyline 25 mg at night — should have stopped on 12 October.\nAlendronic acid 70 mg weekly — taken incorrectly, as above.\nColecalciferol daily — as intended.\nAtorvastatin 20 mg at night — as intended.\nParacetamol as required — she takes 1 g four times daily every day, which she does not consider to be medication.\nTrimethoprim prophylaxis — not started.\nAn antacid bought over the counter, taken most mornings.\nNo allergies recorded on our patient medication record, and none reported by her.\n\nOther findings:\nBlood pressure taken by her daughter at home last week: 108/62 sitting, and she reports feeling \"swimmy\" on standing since coming home. She has had one further near-fall on 21 October, not reported to anyone before today.\nShe is drinking approximately one litre daily and is reluctant to drink more because of the furosemide.\n\nAction already taken by this pharmacy:\nAmitriptyline removed from the dosette box and the remaining supply retained.\nThe furosemide discrepancy has NOT been changed by me, as the dose must be corrected on the prescription; she has been advised of the intended dose and has agreed to take one 20 mg tablet from the current supply until the prescription is amended.\nAlendronic acid counselling repeated and a written instruction card given.\nHer daughter has been informed with the patient's consent and was present on 25 October.\n\nRequests to the practice:\nAmend the repeat list to remove the amitriptyline and to correct the furosemide dose.\nAdd the trimethoprim prophylaxis, or confirm that it is no longer intended.\nReview the kidney function and the postural blood pressure, given the thirteen days at the higher dose and the near-fall on 21 October.\nReview the alendronic acid and the regular paracetamol.\nPlease contact me if you would like the discharge summary faxed; I have a copy.",
      "recipient": "Dr Fiona Arkwright, The Selby Road Practice",
      "letterType": "referral",
      "taskInstruction": "Write a letter to the patient's general practitioner setting out the discrepancies found and the action you request."
    }
