    "guidanceNote": "The dispensing record, the symptoms, the technique and the reason he stopped the preventer are the referral. His new job and his football training are not.",
    "payload": {
      "wordMin": 180,
      "wordMax": 200,
      "caseNotes": "HIGHFIELD PHARMACY\nPatient: Mr Jaden Okoye-Marchetti\nAge: 24\nSeen: 2 November 2025, at the counter, when collecting a salbutamol inhaler\nLetter written: 2 November 2025\n\nReason for referral:\nExcessive use of a short-acting reliever inhaler with no preventer being collected, in a young adult with symptomatic asthma.\n\nDispensing record from this pharmacy, verified today:\nSalbutamol 100 microgram inhaler: 14 inhalers issued in the last 12 months, of which 5 in the last 8 weeks. He requested a further two today and I supplied one.\nBeclometasone 200 microgram inhaler: last dispensed 3 March 2025. None since — eight months.\nNo spacer has ever been dispensed to him from this pharmacy.\nNo oral corticosteroid has been dispensed in the last 12 months, and he reports none from any other source.\n\nSymptoms on direct questioning today:\nWakes at night with cough or wheeze three or four nights a week, and has done for about two months.\nUses the reliever inhaler on waking every morning and before any physical activity.\nEstimates using it eight to ten times a day at present.\nChest tightness on cold mornings and when he walks up the hill to work.\nHas stopped playing five-a-side football because of breathlessness, having played weekly for years.\nNo fever, no sputum, no chest pain, no weight loss.\nHe has not seen anybody about his asthma since March and has not attended an annual review.\n\nInhaler technique, observed today with a placebo device:\nHe does not shake the inhaler. He actuates twice in rapid succession while inhaling.\nHis inhalation is fast and short rather than slow and deep, and he does not hold his breath afterwards.\nHe was not aware that a spacer existed.\n\nWhy he stopped the preventer, in his own words:\nHe believed the brown inhaler was \"for when it gets bad\" and that the blue one was the treatment.\nHe said the brown one \"did nothing\" because he felt no difference after using it, which is what he had expected from the blue inhaler.\nNobody had explained that the preventer works over weeks and must be taken when he is well.\nHe also mentioned an unpleasant taste and hoarseness, and did not know that rinsing his mouth would help; he stopped for that reason as much as any other.\n\nOther relevant information:\nNo known allergies. No other regular medication.\nNon-smoker. Vapes daily, which he did not initially describe as smoking and which I asked about directly.\nLives in a rented flat; he reports damp in the bedroom and mould on the window frame.\nWorks as a warehouse operative, indoors, with no known occupational exposure.\nHe has a cat, acquired in August, which sleeps on his bed.\nNo hospital admission for asthma. No emergency department attendance. No previous intensive care.\n\nPeak flow:\nMeasured in the consultation room today with a disposable meter, best of three: 380 litres per minute. His predicted value for his height and age is approximately 630. He had used his reliever inhaler about forty minutes earlier.\nHe has never owned a peak flow meter and has never been asked to keep a diary.\n\nUnderstanding and beliefs:\nHe believes asthma is something people grow out of and that his has \"mostly gone\" because he was worse as a child.\nHe was unaware that using more than three reliever inhalers a year indicates that asthma is not controlled, and he was visibly surprised when shown his own dispensing record.\nHe asked whether the inhalers could be doing him harm, which is a question better answered by you with his full history in front of you.\n\nAction taken today:\nOne salbutamol inhaler supplied; the second request declined pending review, and the reason explained to him.\nInhaler technique corrected and demonstrated; a spacer supplied and its use taught.\nAdvice given on rinsing the mouth after a preventer inhaler.\nHe was advised to attend urgently and has agreed; he asked me to write rather than telephone.\nThe signs that require immediate help were explained and written down for him.\n\nRequest:\nUrgent asthma review, including consideration of restarting preventer treatment, a written asthma action plan, and assessment of the housing and animal exposure.\nHe works shifts and finishes at two o'clock on Tuesdays and Thursdays.",
      "recipient": "Dr Peter Nwaobi, The Highfield Surgery",
      "letterType": "referral",
      "taskInstruction": "Write a referral letter to the patient's general practitioner requesting an asthma review."
    }
