      "caseNotes": "MARSH LANE PHARMACY — STRUCTURED MEDICATION REVIEW SERVICE\nPatient: Mrs Josephine Ekwueme-Halloran\nAge: 84\nReviewed: 8 and 22 June 2025, at home\nLetter written: 22 June 2025\n\nReason for this letter:\nThree separate medicines-related risks identified at a home review, in a patient with two falls and a recent hospital attendance.\n\nCurrent medication, verified from the boxes on 22 June:\nApixaban 5 mg twice daily — atrial fibrillation, since 2021.\nAspirin 75 mg daily — started 2008 after a transient ischaemic attack, never reviewed since the apixaban was started.\nNaproxen 250 mg twice daily — prescribed for knee pain in 2020, on repeat, no gastric protection.\nSertraline 50 mg daily — since 2018.\nAmitriptyline 25 mg at night — for \"sleep and pain\", since 2016.\nOxybutynin 5 mg twice daily — for urinary frequency, since 2017.\nFurosemide 40 mg each morning. Amlodipine 10 mg daily. Ramipril 5 mg daily.\nAtorvastatin 20 mg at night. Levothyroxine 75 mcg daily. Colecalciferol daily.\nParacetamol 1 g four times daily, taken regularly.\nAllergies: none recorded and none reported.\n\nRisk 1 — bleeding.\nShe is taking an anticoagulant, an antiplatelet, a non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug and a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor at the same time, with no gastric protection.\nEach increases bleeding risk and the combination compounds it.\nShe reports bruising easily, two nosebleeds in the last month, and black stools \"once or twice\" which she had not reported to anyone. Her haemoglobin at the emergency department on 2 June was 104, against 128 in 2023.\n\nRisk 2 — anticholinergic burden.\nAmitriptyline and oxybutynin together give a substantial anticholinergic load in a woman of 84.\nShe reports a persistently dry mouth, constipation requiring regular laxatives, blurred vision when reading, and daytime drowsiness.\nHer daughter reports that she has become forgetful over the past two years and repeats herself; no cognitive assessment has been done.\nThe oxybutynin has not improved her urinary symptoms by her own account, and she still passes urine three times a night.\n\nRisk 3 — falls.\nTwo falls in four months, one resulting in the emergency department attendance on 2 June with a head injury and no fracture.\nBlood pressure measured by me on 22 June: 138/76 lying, 106/62 standing at three minutes, with reported light-headedness.\nShe is on three antihypertensives plus a diuretic, and on two sedating medicines.\n\nAdherence and practical findings:\nShe uses a weekly dosette box which her daughter fills from the boxes; the paracetamol and the naproxen are kept separately in the kitchen and taken \"as needed\", which in practice is daily.\nTwo expired boxes of a previous antihypertensive were found in the cupboard and removed with her agreement.\nShe was unable to say what four of her medicines were for.\n\nWhat I propose, for your consideration:\nStop the aspirin — the indication ceased when the apixaban was started.\nStop the naproxen and manage the knee with paracetamol, topical treatment and physiotherapy; if a non-steroidal is essential, add gastric protection.\nReduce and stop the amitriptyline in steps, with a plan for the sleep and the pain.\nReview the oxybutynin, which appears ineffective and is contributing to the burden.\nReview the antihypertensive load in view of the postural drop.\nCheck the full blood count and ferritin given the haemoglobin and the reported black stools.\nConsider a cognitive assessment once the anticholinergic burden has been reduced rather than before.\n\nHer own view:\nShe said she would \"take whatever the doctor says\", and then, when asked directly which tablets she would give up if she could, she named the one for her waterworks and the one that makes her mouth dry — which are the two I would have chosen.\nShe is anxious about stopping the amitriptyline because she associates it with sleeping at all, and this will need a slow reduction and a plan rather than a single decision.\n\nWhat I have already done:\nExplained each proposed change to her and to her daughter, who was present on 22 June.\nAdvised her to stop the naproxen today pending your review, which she has done.\nAdvised her to report black stools immediately and given written information.\nRemoved the expired medicines.\nShe and her daughter have both agreed to a review and would prefer a home visit.",
      "recipient": "Dr Stephen Iwuchukwu, The Marsh Lane Practice",
