      "caseNotes": "FERNDALE PHARMACY\nPatient: Mr Hubert Nkemelu-Carstairs\nAge: 81\nSeen: 3 and 10 September 2025, at home during medicine deliveries\nLetter written: 10 September 2025\n\nReason for this letter:\nTwo insulin devices being confused at home, with evidence of both hypoglycaemia and missed doses, in a man who lives alone and is managing his own injections.\n\nBackground:\nType 2 diabetes for 22 years, on insulin since 2015.\nPrescribed: insulin glargine 100 units/ml, 24 units at bedtime, and insulin aspart 100 units/ml, 8 units with each main meal.\nBoth are supplied as pre-filled pens. The pens are similar in size and both are white with coloured markings; the boxes are stored together in the door of his refrigerator.\nHe lives alone. His son visits at weekends. No care package.\n\nWhat I found on 3 September:\nHe described his routine as \"the long one at night and the quick one with dinner\", but when asked to show me, he picked up the rapid-acting pen and said it was the night one.\nTwo used pens were in the bin; one was the bedtime insulin and had been used for four days rather than the expected fourteen, which suggests it has been used for meals as well.\nAn unopened box of the bedtime insulin was six weeks past its supply date, indicating it has not been used at the expected rate.\nHis blood glucose diary shows: readings of 3.1, 3.4 and 2.9 at approximately eleven in the evening on three separate nights in the last fortnight, and readings of 18 to 22 before breakfast on five mornings.\nHe treated the low readings with biscuits and did not report them to anybody.\nHe has had two episodes of sweating and confusion in the night, described by his son.\n\nVision and dexterity:\nRegistered as partially sighted in 2023, with macular degeneration. He cannot read the numbers on the dial and sets the dose by counting clicks, which he was never taught to do and which he described to me himself.\nHe has a magnifier supplied in 2023 which he no longer uses because \"it is more trouble\".\nMarked tremor of both hands, worse when concentrating.\nHe was unable to distinguish the two pens by touch or by colour when tested, and identified them correctly on one of four attempts.\n\nInjection technique:\nInjects into the same area of the left abdomen every time; there is a firm, raised area of approximately 6 cm — lipohypertrophy — which he says \"does not hurt so I use it\".\nDoes not rotate sites. Reuses needles, changing them approximately weekly.\nDoes not prime the pen before injecting.\n\nAction taken by this pharmacy:\nBoth insulins have been dispensed today in clearly differentiated packaging, with a large-print label on each pen body, one wrapped with a textured band so that the two can be told apart by touch.\nThe bedtime insulin now has a raised marker; the mealtime pen does not.\nNew needles supplied and single use explained.\nSite rotation explained and a diagram left with him.\nHis son was telephoned with consent on 10 September and is willing to attend a teaching session.\nI have not changed any dose and have advised him to continue as prescribed until reviewed.\n\nOther medication and relevant history:\nMetformin 1 g twice daily. Ramipril 5 mg daily. Atorvastatin 40 mg at night. Aspirin 75 mg daily.\nFurosemide 20 mg each morning.\nChronic kidney disease stage 3, creatinine 132 in June.\nNo allergies known. Non-smoker. Does not drive.\nHe eats one cooked meal a day, delivered, at about half past twelve, and has toast in the evening — which is relevant to the mealtime doses he has been advised to take three times a day.\n\nRequest:\nAn assessment of whether he can continue to self-administer safely, and if so what support he needs; if not, a district nursing visit for the bedtime dose at least.\nI would also ask for the lipohypertrophy to be examined, since injecting into it is likely to explain some of the erratic readings, and for the hypoglycaemia at night to be reported to his diabetes team.\nHe is at home every day and prefers visits before midday.",
