      "caseNotes": "OLD VICARAGE PHARMACY\nPatient: Mr Lionel Ferrant-Adeyemo\nAge: 52\nSeen: 14 and 28 November 2025\nLetter written: 28 November 2025\n\nReason for this letter:\nEscalating opioid use over four years for chronic low back pain, with features suggesting dependence and probable medication-overuse headache, in a patient who has raised it himself.\n\nDispensing history from this pharmacy:\n2021 — co-codamol 30/500, two tablets four times daily, one box per month.\n2022 — the same, with an additional box requested early on four occasions.\n2023 — dose increased to include tramadol 50 mg as required; both dispensed monthly.\n2024 — tramadol changed to modified release 100 mg twice daily, with co-codamol continued.\n2025 — the current position: co-codamol 30/500 eight tablets daily, tramadol modified release 200 mg twice daily, and eleven early requests in the last twelve months, of which three were refused by the practice and eight were issued.\nTotal daily oral morphine equivalent, as I have calculated it: approximately 120 mg.\n\nOver-the-counter purchases, disclosed by him on 28 November:\nHe has been buying codeine-containing tablets from three other pharmacies, approximately one packet a week for at least eighteen months, taking them in addition to the prescribed medicines.\nHe said he buys from different shops so that nobody asks.\nThis has never appeared on any prescription record.\n\nSymptoms he reports:\nHeadache present on most mornings for the past year, relieved temporarily by taking the tablets, which is why he takes them early.\nConstipation requiring daily laxatives; three episodes of impaction needing intervention in 2025.\nDaytime drowsiness, and he has stopped driving on his own initiative after nearly falling asleep at a junction in September.\nLow mood, poor sleep, and sweating and restlessness if he is late with a dose — which he described without prompting and which he called \"the shakes\".\nReduced appetite; weight down from 92 kg to 84 kg over two years.\nReduced libido and fatigue, which he mentioned with some difficulty.\n\nThe pain itself:\nThe original problem was a lumbar disc prolapse in 2017, managed without surgery.\nHe reports that his pain today is \"about the same as it was in 2018\", despite the dose having roughly tripled since then.\nHe has never been referred to a pain service. Physiotherapy was offered in 2018 and he attended twice.\nHe does not exercise and describes his day as mostly sitting.\n\nWhat he has said:\nOn 28 November, unprompted, he said: \"I don't think they're doing anything any more but I can't stop them.\" He asked whether he could be helped to come off them and asked me not to tell his employer.\nHe is frightened of the pain returning and more frightened of the withdrawal.\nHe has not discussed any of this with the practice because he assumed the prescriptions would simply be stopped.\n\nOther medication:\nAmitriptyline 25 mg at night. Omeprazole 20 mg daily. Senna and macrogol daily.\nAllergies: none known. Alcohol: he reports four to six units at weekends only.\nHe works full time as a security supervisor, mostly seated, and lives with his partner.\n\nAction taken by this pharmacy:\nI have not refused any prescription and have made no change to what he is taking.\nI have explained medication-overuse headache and how it produces exactly the morning pattern he describes.\nI have explained that a reduction is possible, that it is done slowly and by agreement, and that nobody would stop his prescription abruptly.\nHe has agreed to this letter and has agreed to attend. He has asked whether he could see somebody who does this specifically rather than only his doctor.\nI have entered the over-the-counter purchasing on his patient medication record with his consent.\n\nRequest:\nA review with a view to a planned, gradual reduction, and referral to the pain service and to the local substance misuse service if you think it appropriate.\nHe is available on Mondays and Fridays and has asked for a longer appointment, and he has asked that his partner be able to come with him.",
