    "profession": "PHYSIOTHERAPY",
    "slug": "wri-physiotherapy-referral-to-rheumatology-suspected-inflammatory-back-pain",
    "title": "Physiotherapy — Referral to rheumatology: suspected inflammatory back pain",
    "prompt": "Using the case notes, write a letter as instructed. Write 180–200 words. Select only the information that is relevant to the reader and to the purpose of the letter.",
    "difficulty": "CORE",
    "topicTag": "rheumatology",
    "timeLimitSeconds": 2700,
    "active": true,
    "guidanceNote": "The pattern of the pain, the features that distinguish it from mechanical back pain, the family history and the eye episode are the referral. His football and his job title are not.",
    "payload": {
      "wordMin": 180,
      "wordMax": 200,
      "caseNotes": "FAIRHOLME MUSCULOSKELETAL PHYSIOTHERAPY\nPatient: Mr Tobias Nwankwo-Hargreave\nAge: 26\nAppointments: 8 and 29 April 2025\nReferral written: 29 April 2025\n\nReason for referral:\nA pattern of back pain that I believe is inflammatory rather than mechanical, in a young man with a relevant family history and a previous episode of eye inflammation.\n\nWhy he was referred to physiotherapy:\nReferred by his general practitioner with \"mechanical low back pain, for core stability work\", following fourteen months of symptoms.\n\nThe pattern of the pain, as elicited on 8 April:\nOnset gradual, at the age of 25, with no injury and no lifting event.\nDuration fourteen months, continuous rather than episodic.\nWorse with REST and better with MOVEMENT — he paces the kitchen at four in the morning because lying still is intolerable.\nMorning stiffness lasting two to three hours before he can bend properly. He gets up an hour early for this and has done for a year.\nNight pain in the second half of the night, waking him at three or four every night, and improving once he is up and walking.\nAlternating buttock pain, left one week and right the next, which he offered without prompting.\nImproves substantially with anti-inflammatory tablets and returns within a day of stopping them.\nSitting for long periods makes it worse; a long drive is his worst activity.\n\nFeatures that argue against a mechanical cause:\nNo injury, no lifting event, no trauma at any point.\nBetter with exercise, worse with rest — the reverse of the pattern he was referred with.\nFourteen months without any pain-free interval.\nProlonged early morning stiffness, which is the finding that changed my thinking.\nResponse to anti-inflammatory medication that is dramatic and reproducible.\n\nExamination, 29 April:\nLumbar flexion: modified Schober test 3.5 cm — reduced.\nLumbar side flexion reduced bilaterally, right 14 cm and left 15 cm finger-to-floor.\nChest expansion 3.5 cm at the fourth intercostal space — reduced for a man of his age and build.\nOcciput to wall 0 cm; no cervical involvement.\nSacroiliac provocation tests positive bilaterally on three of five tests.\nStraight leg raise 80 degrees bilaterally with no reproduction of leg pain.\nNeurological examination of both legs entirely normal: power 5 out of 5 throughout, reflexes normal and symmetrical, sensation intact.\nNo swelling of any peripheral joint today. No dactylitis. No enthesitis at the Achilles or the plantar fascia on palpation, though he reports heel pain in the mornings that settles.\n\nOther history that emerged on direct questioning:\nAn episode of a red, painful, light-sensitive left eye in 2023, treated with drops at an eye casualty department, diagnosis recorded by him as \"iritis\". This resolved and he had not connected it with his back.\nNo psoriasis. No inflammatory bowel symptoms: no diarrhoea, no blood in the stool, no weight loss.\nNo urethral symptoms. No recent infection.\nFamily history: his father has ankylosing spondylitis, diagnosed in his thirties, and his paternal aunt has psoriasis. He had not been asked about this before.\n\nTreatment given here, and why I am stopping it:\nTwo sessions of exercise and posture advice.\nI have not continued with a core stability programme, because the diagnosis he was referred with does not fit what I have found, and a further six weeks of physiotherapy would delay this referral rather than treat him.\nI have given him an exercise and mobility programme, which is appropriate whatever the diagnosis, and which I have explained will remain part of his treatment.\n\nWhat he has been told:\nThat the pattern of his symptoms does not fit the diagnosis he was referred with, and that there is a recognised condition which fits it well.\nThat his father's diagnosis is relevant and that this is worth telling any clinician who sees him.\nThat I am not the person who makes the diagnosis, and that the investigations will be arranged by your service.\n\nRequest:\nAssessment for axial spondyloarthritis, including the investigations your service uses.\nHe is 26, has had symptoms for fourteen months, and has had no diagnosis in that time.\nHe works as a delivery driver, sits for long periods, and has taken eleven days off in six months.\nHe can attend at any time and has asked to be seen as soon as possible.",
      "recipient": "Dr Yolanda Prescott-Ibeh, Consultant Rheumatologist, Fairholme Hospital",
      "letterType": "referral",
      "taskInstruction": "Write a referral letter to the consultant rheumatologist."
    }
