    "subTest": "WRITING",
    "taskType": "WRITING_LETTER",
    "profession": "PHYSIOTHERAPY",
    "slug": "wri-physiotherapy-referral-to-orthopaedics-a-shoulder-that-has-not-responded",
    "title": "Physiotherapy — Referral to orthopaedics: a shoulder that has not responded",
    "prompt": "Using the case notes, write a letter as instructed. Write 180–200 words. Select only the information that is relevant to the reader and to the purpose of the letter.",
    "difficulty": "CORE",
    "topicTag": "musculoskeletal",
    "timeLimitSeconds": 2700,
    "active": true,
    "guidanceNote": "The measured findings, what treatment has been given and for how long, the imaging and the effect on her work are the referral. Her tennis club and the department's staffing are not.",
    "payload": {
      "wordMin": 180,
      "wordMax": 200,
      "caseNotes": "WEYBOURNE MUSCULOSKELETAL PHYSIOTHERAPY\nPatient: Mrs Constance Adeyemi-Locke\nAge: 58\nSessions: 10 sessions, 14 January to 6 May 2025\nReferral written: 6 May 2025\n\nReason for referral:\nA right shoulder that has not responded to sixteen weeks of structured physiotherapy, with findings suggesting a rotator cuff tear rather than tendinopathy.\n\nHistory:\nGradual onset in October 2024, with no specific injury; she recalls reaching behind her into the back seat of a car and feeling a \"tearing\" sensation, which she had forgotten until asked directly.\nPain on the outer upper arm, radiating to the deltoid insertion, not below the elbow.\nNight pain: unable to lie on the right side; waking three or four times nightly, which has been constant throughout.\nUnable to reach behind her back to fasten clothing, or to reach overhead into a cupboard.\nPain 7 out of 10 at night, 5 on movement, 2 at rest.\n\nFindings at assessment, 14 January:\nActive abduction 90 degrees, with a painful arc between 70 and 110 degrees.\nPassive abduction 150 degrees — the difference between active and passive is the finding I would emphasise.\nActive external rotation 30 degrees, passive 50 degrees.\nEmpty can test positive and weak. External rotation strength 3 out of 5 on the right, 5 on the left.\nPainful and weak resisted external rotation, with a positive external rotation lag sign of 20 degrees.\nNo cervical component: full cervical range, no reproduction of shoulder pain on cervical movement, negative Spurling test, normal upper limb neurology.\nNo instability. No acromioclavicular tenderness. Scapular control adequate.\n\nFindings today, 6 May, after sixteen weeks:\nActive abduction 95 degrees. Passive 150 degrees. Painful arc unchanged.\nExternal rotation strength 3 out of 5 — UNCHANGED after sixteen weeks of progressive loading.\nExternal rotation lag sign still positive at 20 degrees.\nNight pain unchanged; she still cannot lie on that side.\nPain scores unchanged.\nThe only measure that has improved is her scapular control, which was not the problem.\n\nTreatment given:\nTen sessions over sixteen weeks.\nWeeks 1 to 4: pain modulation, isometric loading, scapular work, activity modification.\nWeeks 5 to 10: progressive resisted external rotation and abduction, a home programme three times weekly which she has completed reliably — she brought her diary and had missed four sessions in sixteen weeks.\nWeeks 11 to 16: continued progression with no change in strength or in lag.\nA subacromial corticosteroid injection was given by her general practitioner on 3 March. It reduced the pain for approximately ten days and had no effect on the weakness.\n\nImaging:\nPlain radiograph, 20 January: no fracture, no significant acromioclavicular arthritis, no calcification. Acromiohumeral distance appears reduced, reported as 6 mm.\nUltrasound, 24 March, report enclosed: full-thickness tear of the supraspinatus tendon measuring approximately 14 mm in the anterior to posterior dimension, with retraction. Infraspinatus intact.\nSubscapularis intact. Long head of biceps intact.\n\nFunction and work:\nShe works as a hairdresser and has been unable to work above shoulder height since January.\nShe has reduced from five days to three and has lost income.\nShe cannot dry her own hair or fasten a bra without help.\nShe has not driven at night since February because of the sleep loss.\n\nMedical background:\nType 2 diabetes, HbA1c 54 in February — relevant to healing and to any further injection.\nHypertension. Non-smoker. Body mass index 27.\nNo previous shoulder surgery, no previous injection before March, no inflammatory arthritis.\nMedication: metformin, ramipril, atorvastatin, paracetamol and ibuprofen as required.\nAllergies: none known.\n\nRequest:\nAn orthopaedic opinion on a full-thickness supraspinatus tear that has not responded to an adequate course of physiotherapy, with a persistent external rotation lag and unchanged strength.\nShe is aware that surgery is one possibility and has asked what the alternative is if it is not offered; she would value that answered directly.\nShe can attend at any time and does not need transport, and she has asked whether continuing the exercises in the meantime would do any harm, which I have told her it would not.",
      "recipient": "Mr Douglas Ferreira-Nkemdi, Consultant Orthopaedic Surgeon, Shoulder Service, Weybourne Hospital",
      "letterType": "referral",
      "taskInstruction": "Write a referral letter to the consultant orthopaedic surgeon."
    }
