    "slug": "wri-physiotherapy-referral-to-the-pain-service-persistent-low-back-pain",
    "title": "Physiotherapy — Referral to the pain service: persistent low back pain",
    "prompt": "Using the case notes, write a letter as instructed. Write 180–200 words. Select only the information that is relevant to the reader and to the purpose of the letter.",
    "difficulty": "CORE",
    "topicTag": "persistent-pain",
    "timeLimitSeconds": 2700,
    "active": true,
    "guidanceNote": "The duration, the treatments tried and their effect, the current medication, the beliefs and the effect on her life are the referral. Her cat and the department's waiting times are not.",
    "payload": {
      "wordMin": 180,
      "wordMax": 200,
      "caseNotes": "RAVENSMOOR MUSCULOSKELETAL PHYSIOTHERAPY\nPatient: Miss Rosalind Ekechukwu-Vance\nAge: 39\nSessions: 14 sessions across three episodes of care, 2022 to 2025\nReferral written: 22 June 2025\n\nReason for referral:\nPersistent low back pain of five years' duration, now with a substantial effect on work and mood, after three episodes of physiotherapy and no identified structural cause requiring intervention.\n\nHistory:\nOnset in 2020 after a fall from a horse. Initial imaging at the time showed no fracture.\nMRI in 2021: mild disc degeneration at L4/5 and L5/S1, no nerve root compression, no stenosis, no inflammatory change. Reported as \"age-appropriate changes\".\nReviewed by orthopaedics in 2022 and discharged with no surgical option, which she understood as \"nothing is wrong\".\nPain is constant, present every day for five years, described as a deep ache across the low back with intermittent spread into both buttocks.\nPain score 6 to 7 out of 10 on most days, 9 on a bad day, and never below 4.\nNo radicular pain. No neurological symptoms. No red flags at any assessment, checked again today.\n\nTreatments tried:\nPhysiotherapy: three episodes — 2022 (six sessions), 2023 (four sessions), 2025 (four sessions).\nEach produced a short-term improvement of about two weeks and no lasting change.\nPilates classes for eight months in 2023, stopped because a flare-up after a class convinced her it was harmful.\nAcupuncture privately, twelve sessions in 2024, no lasting benefit.\nTwo courses of oral steroids from her general practitioner, no benefit.\nA facet joint injection in November 2024: three weeks of relief, then return to baseline.\n\nCurrent medication:\nAmitriptyline 50 mg at night, since 2022.\nCodeine 30 mg, four times daily, since 2023 — she describes needing it to \"get going\".\nNaproxen 500 mg twice daily with omeprazole, since 2021, continuous.\nParacetamol 1 g four times daily.\nShe has increased the codeine herself on bad weeks, up to six doses.\nAllergies: none known.\n\nThe findings that concern me most:\nShe has stopped almost all physical activity. She stopped swimming in 2023 and walking beyond the shops in 2024.\nShe believes that her spine is \"crumbling\" and that movement causes further damage — this belief dates from the 2021 report, which she read herself.\nFear-avoidance beliefs questionnaire: physical activity subscale 22 out of 24.\nShe has reduced her hours from full time to three days a week and has been told informally that a further reduction would put her job at risk.\nSleep: waking three or four times, averaging five hours.\nMood: she declined to complete a mood questionnaire but said, unprompted, that she \"doesn't see the point of anything much\", and that she has stopped seeing friends. She has no thoughts of self-harm on direct questioning and agreed to my telling her general practitioner, which I have done today.\n\nExamination, 22 June:\nLumbar movements: flexion to mid-shin, guarded throughout, with a marked catch on returning to upright which is consistent across repetitions.\nExtension limited and painful. Side flexion symmetrical.\nStraight leg raise 70 degrees bilaterally, no reproduction of leg symptoms.\nNeurological examination of both legs normal: power, reflexes and sensation intact and symmetrical.\nNo spinal tenderness on palpation of the spinous processes; diffuse tenderness across the paraspinal muscles bilaterally.\nShe was able to sit for 40 minutes during the assessment without difficulty and stood for the last ten minutes of it — a discrepancy I mention not as doubt but because it is useful information about what she can actually do.\n\nWhy I am referring rather than continuing:\nA fourth episode of the same physiotherapy would produce a fourth two-week improvement.\nThe maintaining factors now are the medication, the beliefs, the inactivity and the mood, and those need a service that can address them together.\n\nRequest:\nAssessment by the pain service, including a medication review — particularly the codeine and the continuous naproxen — and consideration of a pain management programme.\nShe has agreed to the referral and has asked whether it means she will be told the pain is imaginary; I have told her it does not, and I would ask that this be addressed early.",
      "recipient": "Dr Katarzyna Obi-Fennimore, Consultant in Pain Medicine, Ravensmoor Pain Service",
      "letterType": "referral",
      "taskInstruction": "Write a referral letter to the consultant in pain medicine."
    }
