    "subTest": "WRITING",
    "taskType": "WRITING_LETTER",
    "profession": "RADIOGRAPHY",
    "slug": "wri-radiography-letter-to-the-paediatric-team-repeated-imaging-in-a-child",
    "title": "Radiography — Letter to the paediatric team: repeated imaging in a child",
    "prompt": "Using the case notes, write a letter as instructed. Write 180–200 words. Select only the information that is relevant to the reader and to the purpose of the letter.",
    "difficulty": "CORE",
    "topicTag": "radiation-protection",
    "timeLimitSeconds": 2700,
    "active": true,
    "guidanceNote": "The imaging record, the justification question, the alternatives and what you are asking for are the letter. Criticism of individual referrers is not, and should not appear.",
    "payload": {
      "wordMin": 180,
      "wordMax": 200,
      "caseNotes": "WEXBRIDGE CHILDREN'S HOSPITAL — RADIOLOGY DEPARTMENT\nPatient: Master Aurelio Fitzgibbon-Nnamdi\nAge: 6 years and 2 months\nRequest received: 14 May 2025\nLetter written: 14 May 2025\n\nThe request that prompted this letter:\nCT of the abdomen with contrast, requested today from the paediatric assessment unit.\nClinical question: recurrent abdominal pain, query intussusception.\nI have not cancelled the request and I am not refusing it. I am asking for a conversation before it proceeds, because of the record set out below.\n\nHis imaging in the last fourteen months:\nCT abdomen with contrast, 9 April 2024 — query appendicitis. Reported as normal.\nCT abdomen with contrast, 2 September 2024 — recurrent pain. Reported as normal.\nCT abdomen with contrast, 19 January 2025 — recurrent pain. Small volume of free fluid, otherwise normal.\nAbdominal radiographs on eleven separate occasions in the same period.\nChest radiographs on four occasions.\nUltrasound of the abdomen on two occasions, both normal, the most recent on 3 January 2025.\nNo MRI has been performed at any point.\n\nEstimated dose:\nCumulative effective dose from the three abdominal CT examinations, using our departmental dose-length products and the age-appropriate conversion factor for a six-year-old: approximately 14 millisieverts.\nThe radiographs add approximately a further 1.5 millisieverts.\nTotal in the region of 15.5 millisieverts before today's request, which would add approximately 4 to 5 more.\nFor context in the letter you may wish to write, natural background radiation in this region is approximately 2.2 millisieverts a year.\nThe relevant point is not any single examination, each of which was reasonably requested, but the total in a child of six, whose tissues are more radiosensitive than an adult's and who has many decades of life ahead.\n\nThe clinical picture, as far as the department can see it:\nThree of the requests have come from the paediatric assessment unit, two from the emergency department and one from a surgical registrar. The requests appear not to have been aware of one another, and each states that the pain is recurrent and that previous imaging was normal.\nNo unifying diagnosis appears in the correspondence available to us.\nHe has been seen four times in the last six weeks.\n\nWhat I would propose, for your consideration:\nThat a single named clinician takes ownership of his imaging, so that each attendance is not separately investigated from the beginning.\nThat ultrasound is used as the first-line investigation for intussusception, which it answers well in a child of this age, rather than CT. Our department can provide a paediatric-experienced sonographer within the hour during working hours, and today's question would be better answered that way.\nThat MRI enterography be considered if inflammatory bowel disease is in the differential, since it carries no ionising radiation. He would likely need play preparation rather than anaesthesia at six.\nThat a summary of his imaging to date is placed prominently in his record so that future referrers can see it.\nThat, if further CT is genuinely required, we plan the protocol jointly so that a single examination answers more than one question.\n\nWhat I have done today:\nSpoken to the paediatric registrar on the unit, who has agreed to ultrasound first, and the scan is booked for 15:30 today. Today's CT request is held rather than cancelled.\nCompiled the imaging and dose summary above and placed it in his electronic record.\nReferred the cumulative dose to our medical physics expert for a formal patient dose assessment, in accordance with departmental procedure. This is a routine review and not an incident report.\nAsked our paediatric radiologist to review the three previous CT examinations together, which has not previously been done, in case a pattern is visible across them that was not visible in any one.\n\nWhat I would ask of you:\nA view on whether a coordinated diagnostic plan can be established, and by whom.\nWhether his parents have been given any explanation of the imaging he has had; they asked one of our radiographers in January whether \"all these scans are safe\" and I do not know what answer they were given by the clinical team.\n\nFamily and social details recorded:\nLives with both parents and two siblings. Attends school; he has missed nineteen days this term.\nNo significant past medical history. No previous surgery. No medication. No known allergy.\nBoth parents attend every appointment. Mother is a healthcare assistant.",
      "recipient": "Dr Casimira Nwosu-Tregarthen, Consultant Paediatrician, Wexbridge Children's Hospital",
      "letterType": "referral",
      "taskInstruction": "Write a letter to the referring paediatrician about the cumulative imaging this child has undergone and request a coordinated plan."
    }
