    "subTest": "WRITING",
    "taskType": "WRITING_LETTER",
    "profession": "RADIOGRAPHY",
    "slug": "wri-radiography-letter-to-the-referring-consultant-failed-mri",
    "title": "Radiography — Letter to the referring consultant: failed MRI",
    "prompt": "Using the case notes, write a letter as instructed. Write 180–200 words. Select only the information that is relevant to the reader and to the purpose of the letter.",
    "difficulty": "CORE",
    "topicTag": "imaging-pathway",
    "timeLimitSeconds": 2700,
    "active": true,
    "guidanceNote": "What was attempted, what happened, what remains unanswered and the options open to her are the letter. The department's scanner list and the cost of the wasted slot are not.",
    "payload": {
      "wordMin": 180,
      "wordMax": 200,
      "caseNotes": "WEXBRIDGE HOSPITAL — MAGNETIC RESONANCE IMAGING DEPARTMENT\nPatient: Mrs Cordelia Nwachukwu-Fairbairn\nAge: 46\nAttended: 18 June 2025, 14:00 appointment\nLetter written: 18 June 2025\n\nExamination requested:\nMRI head and cervical spine, with and without gadolinium contrast.\nClinical question stated on the request card: exclude demyelination; six-week history of right optic neuritis with residual visual loss, and new sensory disturbance of both lower limbs.\nRequested 2 June 2025, priority urgent.\n\nWhat happened at the appointment:\nShe attended on time, accompanied by her husband. Safety questionnaire completed and checked; no contraindication identified. No implants, no surgery, no metalwork, not pregnant, no history of metal-fragment injury.\nShe was cannulated in the left antecubital fossa without difficulty for the contrast injection.\nPositioned supine, head coil applied.\nOn advancing into the bore she asked to be removed after approximately twelve seconds, before any sequence had begun.\nSecond attempt after a fifteen-minute break, with the mirror removed, a cold-air flow directed at her face, her husband's hand on her ankle, and her own music through the headphones. She tolerated approximately forty seconds and asked to be removed again.\nThird attempt with feet-first positioning: not tolerated, as the head coil still required her head to be within the bore.\nAt each attempt she became visibly distressed, described a sensation of not being able to breathe, and was tachypnoeic. Pulse 122 after the second attempt.\nNo sequence of diagnostic quality was acquired. No contrast was administered. The cannula was removed intact and the site checked.\n\nWhat she told us:\nShe has been claustrophobic since childhood and did not disclose it when the appointment was made because she \"thought it would be all right on the day\".\nShe has never had an MRI before. She has avoided lifts for thirty years.\nShe was not told before attending how enclosed the scanner is or how long she would be in it. She had expected an examination lasting a few minutes.\nShe is extremely distressed at having failed the examination and asked repeatedly whether this means she will not be diagnosed.\n\nRelevant history:\nNo previous MRI. Previous CT head in 2021 without difficulty, which she confirms she tolerated.\nNo known allergy. eGFR 96 on bloods of 30 May, so gadolinium is not contraindicated on renal grounds.\nWeight 71 kg. Height 1.64 m. Body habitus not a limiting factor.\nMedication: sertraline 50 mg, started three weeks ago by her general practitioner; no benzodiazepine and no other sedative.\nShe drove herself and her husband to the appointment.\nShe works as a primary school teacher and has three weeks of term remaining.\n\nWhat remains unanswered:\nThe clinical question is entirely unanswered. No imaging of any kind was obtained today.\nI would draw your attention to the fact that a CT head would not answer the demyelination question and that I do not consider it an equivalent substitute.\n\nOptions available, for your decision:\nRescan with oral or intravenous anxiolysis, arranged through her general practitioner or through the day-case unit, with an escort and no driving afterwards. This is the option she herself asked about and appears willing to attempt.\nRescan under general anaesthetic, which would require an anaesthetic assessment and a theatre-supported list; our next such list is 9 July.\nReferral to the wide-bore scanner at Ashcombe Park Hospital. The bore is 70 cm rather than 60 cm. I have spoken to their superintendent, who can offer 26 June, but I would not want to promise her a solution that may fail for the same reason.\nA pre-visit for desensitisation, which our department can offer within a week and which some patients find sufficient on its own.\n\nWhat we have done:\nExplained the options to her and to her husband in the department, and given written information on each.\nProvided the department's direct telephone number and the name of the radiographer she spoke to.\nOffered and arranged a pre-visit on 24 June, without prejudice to whichever route you decide upon.\nRecorded the failed attendance in the radiology information system with the reason, so that no further appointment is issued automatically without a plan.\nCopied this letter to her general practitioner.\n\nWhat I would ask of you:\nA decision on which route to take, and a rewritten request card specifying it, since the current card will otherwise generate an identical appointment.\nIf sedation is chosen, a prescription arranged in advance, as we cannot provide one from the department.\nConsideration of whether the urgency has changed, given that the examination is now likely to be delayed by two to four weeks.",
      "recipient": "Dr Marguerite Okonjo-Beresford, Consultant Neurologist, Wexbridge Hospital",
      "letterType": "referral",
      "taskInstruction": "Write a letter to the referring consultant explaining why the examination could not be completed and requesting a decision on an alternative."
    }
