    "subTest": "WRITING",
    "taskType": "WRITING_LETTER",
    "profession": "VETERINARY_SCIENCE",
    "slug": "wri-veterinary-science-referral-to-a-veterinary-behaviourist",
    "title": "Veterinary Science — Referral to a veterinary behaviourist",
    "prompt": "Using the case notes, write a letter as instructed. Write 180–200 words. Select only the information that is relevant to the reader and to the purpose of the letter.",
    "difficulty": "CORE",
    "topicTag": "behavioural-medicine",
    "timeLimitSeconds": 2700,
    "active": true,
    "guidanceNote": "The behaviour, its triggers and escalation, the medical work-up excluding pain and the household circumstances are the referral. Your opinion of the trainer the family used is not.",
    "payload": {
      "wordMin": 180,
      "wordMax": 200,
      "caseNotes": "MARLBROOK VETERINARY PRACTICE\nPatient: \"Ptolemy\", canine, male entire, German Shepherd Dog crossbreed\nAge: 3 years 2 months\nWeight: 34.7 kg\nOwner: Mrs Seraphina Delacroix-Umeh\nSeen: 6 and 20 January 2025\nReferral written: 20 January 2025\n\nReason for referral:\nEscalating aggression towards visitors and, on one recent occasion, towards a family member. I have excluded the medical causes I am able to exclude, and the case now requires behavioural assessment.\nThis is not a training problem and I have declined to treat it as one.\n\nThe behaviour:\nOnset approximately fourteen months ago, at around 24 months of age.\nInitial presentation: barking at the door and retreating behind the sofa when visitors entered.\nProgression: barking became lunging at approximately nine months ago; lunging became snapping at approximately four months ago.\nBite history: one bite, 11 January 2025, to a family friend who reached towards the dog's collar as he was retreating under a table. Level 3 on the Dunbar scale: puncture wounds, no tearing, treated with antibiotics at the minor injuries unit, no lasting injury. The friend has not made a complaint.\nA second incident, 17 January, directed at the owner's adult son, who attempted to move the dog off a bed. Air snap, no contact.\nCommon features across incidents: all occurred indoors; all involved a person approaching him when he was in a confined space or on furniture; all were preceded by clear avoidance signals which were missed. Freezing, lip licking, whale eye and a low growl were present in every episode by the family's own description, and this is important.\nNo aggression outside the home. No aggression towards other dogs. No resource guarding of food. He has never shown aggression to the two children in the household.\nHandling: he has become progressively difficult to examine in the consulting room and now requires a muzzle, which he tolerates.\n\nMedical investigation:\nFull physical examination, 6 January: unremarkable.\nOrthopaedic examination: no pain on manipulation of hips, stifles, elbows or spine. Hip extension negative. No lameness. Muscle mass symmetrical.\nNeurological examination: normal.\nOphthalmic examination and hearing assessment: no deficits identified.\nHaematology and biochemistry, 6 January: within reference ranges throughout.\nTotal T4: 21 nmol/L, within reference. Free T4 and TSH not measured; I would be guided by you as to whether they are worth pursuing.\nFaecal analysis: negative.\nRadiographs of hips and lumbosacral region under sedation, 20 January: mild coxofemoral remodelling bilaterally, no lumbosacral changes, and no findings I consider sufficient to explain the behaviour.\nHe has been on a trial of a non-steroidal anti-inflammatory for two weeks with no change in the behaviour, which I did specifically to test the pain hypothesis.\n\nHousehold and history:\nAcquired at 14 weeks of age from a private seller, with no known history of the dam and no socialisation record. The family believe he had been kept in an outbuilding.\nHousehold: two adults, an adult son who visits at weekends, and two children aged 9 and 12.\nLayout: a small terraced house. The dog's bed is in the kitchen, which is also the through route to the garden, so he is stepped over many times a day.\nExercise: two walks daily, approximately 90 minutes in total, off lead in a field.\nTraining: attended a group class at 6 months. Since the escalation the family used a trainer for six weeks who recommended confrontational methods; the family stopped after four sessions because the behaviour worsened. I record this as a fact because it is part of the learning history.\nNo punishment currently used. No aversive equipment in the house.\nThe family have been managing by shutting him in the utility room when visitors come, which is what they have done since 11 January.\n\nWhat I am requesting:\nFull behavioural assessment and a diagnosis.\nA treatment and management plan the family can realistically deliver in a small house.\nA view on psychopharmacological support, which I have not started and would not start without your guidance.\nAn honest risk assessment. The family have asked me directly whether he is safe with the children. I told them that I could not answer that and that it is precisely why I am referring, and I would ask that the question be answered plainly when you see them.\n\nOwner position:\nMrs Delacroix-Umeh is not insured for behavioural referral; she is aware of the costs and wishes to proceed.\nShe has said that euthanasia has been suggested to her by two people and that she is not willing to consider it without a proper assessment first. She is not refusing to hear it; she is asking that it be a conclusion rather than a starting point.\n\nEnclosed: all laboratory results, radiographs, incident descriptions written by the family, and two short videos of the doorbell response recorded by the son.",
      "recipient": "Dr Isolde Marchetti-Okonkwo, Veterinary Behaviourist, Ashcombe Veterinary Referrals",
      "letterType": "referral",
      "taskInstruction": "Write a referral letter to the veterinary behaviourist."
    }
