      "wordMax": 200,
      "caseNotes": "MARLBROOK VETERINARY PRACTICE\nPatient: \"Clementine\", canine, female neutered, West Highland White Terrier\nAge: 3 years 7 months\nWeight: 8.9 kg\nOwner: Mrs Beatrix Nwankwo-Ellingham\nUnder care since: April 2023. Most recent examination 12 July 2025\nReferral written: 12 July 2025\n\nHistory:\nPruritus first reported at 11 months of age, in June 2023.\nInitially seasonal, from approximately April to October, with complete resolution over the winter for the first two years.\nSince November 2024 the pruritus has been continuous, with a seasonal worsening still evident.\nDistribution, which has been consistent throughout: face, particularly periocular and perioral; the concave pinnae; both axillae; the ventral abdomen and inguinal region; and all four feet, with interdigital involvement and constant licking of the front paws.\nThe dorsum, the tail head and the perianal region have never been affected. I note this because it argues against flea allergy as the primary cause.\nOwner's pruritus score, recorded at each visit on a 10-point scale: 8, 7, 9, 8, 6, 9 over the last six visits.\nSecondary changes: salivary staining of both front feet, lichenification of both axillae, and hyperpigmentation of the ventral abdomen.\nRecurrent otitis externa: five episodes, all bilateral, all erythroceruminous, all responding to topical treatment and recurring within eight to twelve weeks.\nNo gastrointestinal signs at any time: no vomiting, no diarrhoea, normal faecal consistency and frequency.\nNo systemic illness. Appetite and thirst normal. Weight stable.\n\nDiagnostic work performed:\nSkin scrapes, deep and superficial, on five separate occasions: no ectoparasites identified on any.\nHair plucks and trichograms: broken hairs consistent with self-trauma, no Demodex, no dermatophyte arthrospores.\nFungal culture, June 2023: negative at 21 days.\nCytology from the axillae, on three occasions: Malassezia in significant numbers on two, and cocci with neutrophils on one.\nEar cytology: Malassezia predominant on four of five occasions; rods identified on the most recent episode in May 2025, which is a change and is one of my concerns.\nFlea control: isoxazoline product monthly, year-round, without a missed dose since October 2023.\nHousehold includes one cat, also treated monthly. No fleas or flea dirt found at any examination.\nElimination diet trial: hydrolysed diet, strictly, for ten weeks between January and March 2025. The owner kept a food diary which is enclosed and which I consider reliable; no treats, no flavoured medication, no table scraps. Pruritus score fell from 9 to 7 during the trial and returned to 9 within two weeks of rechallenge, which I consider an equivocal rather than a positive result.\nHaematology and biochemistry, February 2025: unremarkable. Thyroid profile normal.\n\nTreatment history and response:\nAntimicrobial therapy for secondary infection on each occasion, with resolution of the infection but not of the pruritus.\nOclacitinib 0.5 mg/kg twice daily for 14 days then once daily: excellent response initially, with the score falling to 3. Effect diminished after approximately five months and the score is now 6 to 7 on the same dose.\nPrednisolone courses, three, each of 10 to 14 days: good response, but polyuria, polydipsia and notable behavioural change which the owner will not accept again.\nCiclosporin: not trialled.\nLokivetmab: not trialled.\nTopical antiseptic shampoo twice weekly, ongoing, with modest benefit.\nEssential fatty acid supplementation for six months: no discernible effect.\n\nWhat I am requesting:\nIntradermal or serological allergy testing and a view on allergen-specific immunotherapy, which is the principal reason for this referral, given the age of onset and the fourteen-month duration of continuous signs.\nConfirmation or exclusion of a food component, since my diet trial result was equivocal and I would rather it were repeated properly under your supervision than argued about.\nCulture and sensitivity of the ear canals given the rods on cytology, and an otoscopic assessment including the integrity of the tympanic membranes, which I have not been able to visualise on the right for two visits.\nA long-term management plan that this practice can maintain.\n\nOwner circumstances:\nMrs Nwankwo-Ellingham is insured but the policy excludes skin disease from the previous policy year;\nthe exclusion wording is enclosed and she is fully aware of the financial position.\nShe is highly compliant, keeps written records, and has never missed a treatment.\nShe has told me she is exhausted by the scratching at night and that this is affecting her sleep, and that she needs to know whether this is manageable for the next ten years.\n\nEnclosed: all cytology and laboratory results, the food diary, photographs of the affected sites taken in April and July 2025, and the complete clinical record.",
      "recipient": "Dr Wilhelmina Oyelowo-Cadwallader, Specialist in Veterinary Dermatology, Ashcombe Veterinary Referrals",
